{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj92KUTyTYZ6AFdv5v4b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Grw1qAAxAfscomnFfERNdb","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"gh api repos/michaelcrosato/shardfall/contents/docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md -H \"Accept: application/vnd.github.raw\" 2>&1","description":"Fetch the engine's make-a-game guide"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9403,"cache_read_input_tokens":25479,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9403},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33b9a197-b2fe-42e5-9a43-b32ae2dde858","uuid":"4c974700-216e-4148-aec2-319d4067f853","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:06.337Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj92KD6UhCiR1ReJYfkU","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Grw1qAAxAfscomnFfERNdb":{"command":"gh api repos/michaelcrosato/shardfall/contents/docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md -H \"Accept: application/vnd.github.raw\" 2>&1","description":"Fetch the engine's make-a-game guide"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Grw1qAAxAfscomnFfERNdb","type":"tool_result","content":"# Make a game on Pavilion\n\nFor an agent asked to build a game on this engine. Read this page whole (about 3k tokens); it\nis the only one you need to start. Search the others when a step points to them. The engine\nwas made for agents: everything runs, renders and checks headless, and every tool is a CLI\ncommand, a REPL line and an MCP tool.\n\n## What you get\n- **A simulation** on the CPU at 60 ticks a second (`pav_core`): characters that walk, jump,\n  climb and swim, rapier physics, pathfinding, projectiles, zones, procedural animation,\n  props from a library. The same inputs and seed always play the same: snapshots, rewind\n  and replays are exact.\n- **A WebGPU renderer** (`pav_render`, `pav_view`): lighting, shadows, bloom, styles and\n  filters (cel, pixel, painterly...), set per level as data.\n- **Synthesized sound** (`pav_audio`): it plays the simulation's events, no audio files.\n- **Tools** (`pav`): capture a frame, step time, drive the player, run your game's bot over\n  many seeds, record and replay. Images come back as PNGs you can look at.\n- **Three builds** from one source: a window on Linux and Windows, and the browser (also as\n  one self-contained HTML file).\n\nShardfall, the hack-and-slash in `crates/pav_core/src/arpg/`, and the pavilion of demo rooms\nare built on the same engine. They are examples, not starting points: start from the\n**starter game**.\n\n## Read this, skip that\n- **Use:** this page; `crates/pav_core/src/games/` (the hook and the starter, about 600\n  lines); `rooms/_template.toml` (the level format: search it for a field); `docs/REFERENCE.md`\n  \"Tools\" (search for a tool); `pav help`.\n- **`AGENTS.md`** is the rulebook for agents working on the engine and Shardfall. Its\n  \"Key rules\" apply to your game too (below). Its workflow does not: `docs/HISTORY.md`,\n  `docs/PROGRESS.md`, pull requests, escalations and the asset-store plans are the\n  maintainers'. Follow your own task's instructions for commits and delivery.\n- **Skip:** `docs/HISTORY.md`, `docs/ASSET_SYSTEM_PLAN.md`, `docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`,\n  Shardfall's code and data (`arpg/`, `game/`), `pavilion-lite/` (an older, frozen engine).\n  If you need to read engine source to use the engine, say so in your feedback (below).\n\n## Quick start\n```sh\ncargo build -p pav_tools --bin pav                     # first build: a few minutes\nalias pav=./target/debug/pav\npav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=10               # the starter's bot wins 10/10\npav capture scene=starter ticks=120 width=640 height=360 out=out/starter.png\nprintf 'load scene=starter\\ninput move=[1,0] ticks=60\\nplay\\ncapture out=out/a.png\\n' | pav repl\ncargo test -p pav_core --test games                    # the starter's tests\ncargo run -p pav_app -- --scene starter                # the window (WASD; needs a display)\n```\n`START_HERE.md` has the Linux packages to install. Look at captures with your image tool;\nsmall sizes are cheaper. With MCP (`.mcp.json`), the same tools are `mcp__shardfall__*`.\n\n**The edit loop.** Running from the repository's root, `pav` reads your level file at every\n`load` (and `play seeds=`), so changes to the map and to its `[game]` numbers need no rebuild.\nA change to Rust code rebuilds `pav` (about 2 minutes on 4 cores) and a test binary (as long\nagain): batch code edits, and put balance numbers in `[game]`.\n\n## Your game in six steps\n1. **Copy the starter.** `crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs` and `starter.toml` to\n   `<key>.rs` and `<key>.toml` in the same folder. Rename the struct; set `const KEY` to your\n   key (lowercase, no `/`); point `include_str!` at your level.\n2. **Register it.** In `games/mod.rs`: `pub mod <key>;` and one line in `GAMES`:\n   `entry::<mykey::MyGame>(\"One sentence about it.\", mykey::build)`. Now `load scene=<key>`\n   works in the tools, the window (`--scene <key>`) and the browser (`?scene=<key>`).\n3. **Draw the level** in `<key>.toml` (next section), with your rules' numbers in its `[game]`\n   table (the starter's `Rules`). `pav room_check path=<file>` checks it.\n4. **Write the rules** in your `Game` impl (the hook, below).\n5. **Teach the bot** every rule you add (`Game::bot`): it is how you, the tests and the next\n   agent find out whether the game can be won.\n6. **Copy the tests** in `crates/pav_core/tests/games.rs` to `tests/<key>.rs`: the bot wins on\n   ten seeds, a careless player loses, and snapshots, rewind and replays are exact.\n\n## The level: a room file\nA level is a room file (`rooms/_template.toml` documents every field; the pavilion's\n`rooms/*.toml` are working examples). Rooms built this way stand alone, with no wing or\ncorridor. In a room file:\n- `[layout]`: an ASCII map per floor (`[[layout.layer]]`, 1 cell = 1 m; column = x east,\n  row = z south) and a `legend` per character: floor and wall `blocks` (heights, colours,\n  `destructible`, `crumble`, `ghost`), ladders, `prop`s, `label`s, `grass`, trigger `zone`s\n  (`kill`, `water`, `checkpoint`, `conveyor`, `bounce`, `pad`, `camera`, course\n  `start`/`gate`/`finish`) and `marker = \"<name>\"`.\n- **Markers** are your own pieces: `crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\")` lists every\n  marked cell (world positions, map order). `marker = \"player\"` is the start.\n- `[[npc]]`: characters (`kind = biped | spider | lizard | beetle | blob | quadruped`, `look` colours and\n  proportions, `ai = idle | wander | patrol | circle | follow | guard`). Your game can drive\n  them instead (the starter's slimes).\n- `[[object]]`: library props (`asset = \"LIB/core/<name>\"`; `pav assets find=barrel` searches\n  the store, `assets shelf=prop` lists the best), physics objects, `[[joint]]`s and `[[chain]]`s.\n- `[camera]` (`tilt`, `distance`, `yaw`, `ortho`), `[view]` (light, sky, fog, bloom, styles,\n  filters: `pav params prefix=view` lists them), `[params]` (movement and physics while in\n  the level: `pav params`).\n\n- `[game]`: your rules' numbers, free-form; your `build` reads them (`def.game`).\n- `entrance` is required by the format; with a `player` marker a level doesn't use it.\n\nRead the level with `games::level_text(KEY, include_str!(...))`: the file on disk when running\nfrom the repository, else the copy built in (tests, the browser, an executable). The camera\nfollows the player; to see a whole level from above, `camera preset=top` then\n`set path=camera.distance value=30` (raise it for bigger levels) and `capture`.\n\n## The hook: `pav_core::games::Game`\n```rust\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct MyGame { /* the rules' state: score, timers, entity ids, outcome */ }\n\nimpl Game for MyGame {\n    const KEY: &'static str = \"mygame\";\n    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>)\n        -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame>;          // before movement: inputs for characters you drive\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>); // after physics: rules\n    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String>;        // text lines, top left; `play` returns them\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value; // your state for tools and tests\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome;                   // Playing | Won | Lost\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame;         // what a good player would press now\n}\npub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()>; // the scene; sets sim.state.play\n```\nEvery method has a default. `build` gets the rest of a scene name `mygame/<rest>` (a level\nnumber, say). Each tick runs: behaviours, your `pre`, characters move (your inputs replace\ntheir own), physics, shots move and hit, your `post`. While `pre`/`post` run, your game is out\nof `sim.state.play`: use `self` for your state and `sim` for the world. In `post`, `events`\nalready holds this tick's events: read them to react (`Hit { target, .. }` names the\ncharacter a shot struck: it is stunned, knocked back and safe from shots for 0.6 s), and push\nyour own. From outside, `sim.play()?.get::<MyGame>()` reads your game; in a test,\n`sim.state.play.as_mut().unwrap().get_mut::<MyGame>().unwrap()` changes it.\n\n## Building blocks\n| You need | Use |\n|---|---|\n| The player | `sim.state.player`; it walks by the keys' `InputFrame` (`move_dir`, `buttons::JUMP`, `PRIMARY`...); `sim.feet(id)` is where a character stands (`pos` is its centre) |\n| Characters | `[[npc]]` in the level, or `sim.spawn_npc(name, feet, facing, PuppetDef::preset(BodyPlan::Blob), None, None)`; drive them from `pre` |\n| Pickups, hazards, scenery | `sim.spawn(Spawn::new(\"name\", pos).visual(Visual::new(shape, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"))).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed }))`; `sim.despawn(id)`; `sim.set_position(id, feet)` |\n| Moving platforms, emitters, spinners | `Behavior::Move`, `Rotate`, `Emitter`, `Spawner` (`entity.rs`), or `[[object]]` in the level |\n| Shots | `sim.state.projectiles.spawn(Projectile { pos, vel, radius, life, color, knockback, gravity, owner, team, damage })`: plain structs, stopped by walls; `Team::Enemy` shots hit characters (a `Hit` event), `Team::Player` shots hit things with `health` |\n| Pathfinding | `nav::NavGrid::for_room(sim)` (the level's walkable grid); `grid.path(from, to)`; `grid.flow(goal, reach)` steers many chasers at once; `grid.walkable(p)` |\n| Randomness | `sim.state.rng` only (`range`, `below`, `f32`): seeded, so a seed always plays the same |\n| Messages | `sim.say(\"text\")`: centre top for a few seconds |\n| Sound and effects | push `SimEvent`s from `pre`/`post`: `Coin`, `Pickup`, `Hit`, `Explosion`, `Shot`, `Jump`, `Land`... The renderer and the synth play them |\n| Tuning | your numbers: the level's `[game]`; the engine's: `pav params`, `pav set path=movement.speed value=4.5` (movement, physics, camera, view), and a level's `[params]` set them while in it |\n| Looks | the level's `[view]`; `pav look` lists looks and filters; `rooms/mix_match.toml` mixes styles per part |\n| Animation | characters animate procedurally; motion clips: `pav assets shelf=clip`, then `clips = [\"SET/Clip\"]` on an `[[npc]]` |\n\n## Rules that keep it working\n- **Same inputs and seed, same game.** Random numbers come from `sim.state.rng`; never read the\n  clock; never let `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (`BTreeMap`, or sort).\n- **All game state lives in your struct or in entities**, and your struct is `Clone` + serde\n  (snapshots are clones; files and the replay hash are its CBOR). A cache that follows from\n  the level (a nav grid) is `#[serde(skip)]` and rebuilt when missing.\n- **`pav_core` has no window, GPU or audio code**, and game code must run in the browser: no\n  threads, blocking calls or file access (embed data with `include_str!`).\n- **Check with the tools, not by reading engine code.** If a tool or error message didn't tell\n  you what you needed, that is engine feedback.\n\n## Check your work (no screen needed)\n| Question | Command (`pav <tool>` or a `pav repl` line) |\n|---|---|\n| Can it be won? Lost? | `play bot=true seeds=10` (wins, seconds, the state of every run not won) |\n| What's on screen? | `capture width=640 height=360 out=out/a.png`; `filmstrip` for motion in one image; to watch the bot, repeat `play bot=true ticks=30` and `capture` in a `pav repl` |\n| Does a rule fire? | `input move=[1,0] ticks=30`, then `play` (HUD, outcome, `status`) |\n| Where is everything? | `entities`, `player`, `status` (with the state hash) |\n| Same every time? | `record_save path=out/r.json`, then `replay path=out/r.json` (checks the hash); `rewind`, `snapshot_save`/`snapshot_load` |\n| Is the level valid? | `room_check path=crates/pav_core/src/games/<key>.toml` |\n| Does it run fast? | `bench scene=<key> ticks=1200` |\n| Does it still pass? | `cargo test -p pav_core --test <key>`, `cargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo fmt --all` |\n\n## Ship it\n- **Window:** `cargo run -p pav_app -- --scene <key>` (Linux; `xvfb-run -a` without a display).\n  F5 restarts the game (Esc opens the menu).\n- **Windows `.exe`:** `scripts/build-windows.sh` (after `scripts/setup-linux.sh`).\n- **Browser, one file:** `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene <key> --title \"My Game\"\n  --out out/mygame.html`: about 10 MB, runs from `file://` in Chrome or Edge (WebGPU).\n\n## Limits today\n- HUD: text lines only (captures draw them; `ui` reads them). Panels, bars or menus mean egui\n  code in `pav_ui` (draw it from `headless.rs` too, so the tools see it).\n- Drawing from game state without an entity (`pav_view::assets::emit_asset`) and new sounds\n  (`pav_audio`, a closed list) mean code outside `pav_core`.\n- Your game compiles into the engine crate (`pav_core/src/games/`), beside Shardfall.\n- The camera follows the player with the level's `[camera]` settings; there is no\n  per-game camera code. Keep levels about 20 m across, or raise `[camera] distance`.\n- The window's controls card lists the engine demo's keys (bombs, vehicles), not your game's.\n\n## Report back\nWhen you hand the game back, add a short **engine feedback** section, written for an engine\nagent who has never seen your work: what you used, the engine source you had to read and\nwhy, what you wrote that the engine should have had (with the file), wrong or missing docs,\nand the one change that would have helped most. The maintainers log it in\n`docs/FEEDBACK.md`, and what keeps coming back becomes engine work.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33b9a197-b2fe-42e5-9a43-b32ae2dde858","uuid":"4e7fb4e5-5856-42c3-bfe6-514897765e6d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:06.807Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Make a game on Pavilion\n\nFor an agent asked to build a game on this engine. Read this page whole (about 3k tokens); it\nis the only one you need to start. Search the others when a step points to them. The engine\nwas made for agents: everything runs, renders and checks headless, and every tool is a CLI\ncommand, a REPL line and an MCP tool.\n\n## What you get\n- **A simulation** on the CPU at 60 ticks a second (`pav_core`): characters that walk, jump,\n  climb and swim, rapier physics, pathfinding, projectiles, zones, procedural animation,\n  props from a library. The same inputs and seed always play the same: snapshots, rewind\n  and replays are exact.\n- **A WebGPU renderer** (`pav_render`, `pav_view`): lighting, shadows, bloom, styles and\n  filters (cel, pixel, painterly...), set per level as data.\n- **Synthesized sound** (`pav_audio`): it plays the simulation's events, no audio files.\n- **Tools** (`pav`): capture a frame, step time, drive the player, run your game's bot over\n  many seeds, record and replay. Images come back as PNGs you can look at.\n- **Three builds** from one source: a window on Linux and Windows, and the browser (also as\n  one self-contained HTML file).\n\nShardfall, the hack-and-slash in `crates/pav_core/src/arpg/`, and the pavilion of demo rooms\nare built on the same engine. They are examples, not starting points: start from the\n**starter game**.\n\n## Read this, skip that\n- **Use:** this page; `crates/pav_core/src/games/` (the hook and the starter, about 600\n  lines); `rooms/_template.toml` (the level format: search it for a field); `docs/REFERENCE.md`\n  \"Tools\" (search for a tool); `pav help`.\n- **`AGENTS.md`** is the rulebook for agents working on the engine and Shardfall. Its\n  \"Key rules\" apply to your game too (below). Its workflow does not: `docs/HISTORY.md`,\n  `docs/PROGRESS.md`, pull requests, escalations and the asset-store plans are the\n  maintainers'. Follow your own task's instructions for commits and delivery.\n- **Skip:** `docs/HISTORY.md`, `docs/ASSET_SYSTEM_PLAN.md`, `docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`,\n  Shardfall's code and data (`arpg/`, `game/`), `pavilion-lite/` (an older, frozen engine).\n  If you need to read engine source to use the engine, say so in your feedback (below).\n\n## Quick start\n```sh\ncargo build -p pav_tools --bin pav                     # first build: a few minutes\nalias pav=./target/debug/pav\npav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=10               # the starter's bot wins 10/10\npav capture scene=starter ticks=120 width=640 height=360 out=out/starter.png\nprintf 'load scene=starter\\ninput move=[1,0] ticks=60\\nplay\\ncapture out=out/a.png\\n' | pav repl\ncargo test -p pav_core --test games                    # the starter's tests\ncargo run -p pav_app -- --scene starter                # the window (WASD; needs a display)\n```\n`START_HERE.md` has the Linux packages to install. Look at captures with your image tool;\nsmall sizes are cheaper. With MCP (`.mcp.json`), the same tools are `mcp__shardfall__*`.\n\n**The edit loop.** Running from the repository's root, `pav` reads your level file at every\n`load` (and `play seeds=`), so changes to the map and to its `[game]` numbers need no rebuild.\nA change to Rust code rebuilds `pav` (about 2 minutes on 4 cores) and a test binary (as long\nagain): batch code edits, and put balance numbers in `[game]`.\n\n## Your game in six steps\n1. **Copy the starter.** `crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs` and `starter.toml` to\n   `<key>.rs` and `<key>.toml` in the same folder. Rename the struct; set `const KEY` to your\n   key (lowercase, no `/`); point `include_str!` at your level.\n2. **Register it.** In `games/mod.rs`: `pub mod <key>;` and one line in `GAMES`:\n   `entry::<mykey::MyGame>(\"One sentence about it.\", mykey::build)`. Now `load scene=<key>`\n   works in the tools, the window (`--scene <key>`) and the browser (`?scene=<key>`).\n3. **Draw the level** in `<key>.toml` (next section), with your rules' numbers in its `[game]`\n   table (the starter's `Rules`). `pav room_check path=<file>` checks it.\n4. **Write the rules** in your `Game` impl (the hook, below).\n5. **Teach the bot** every rule you add (`Game::bot`): it is how you, the tests and the next\n   agent find out whether the game can be won.\n6. **Copy the tests** in `crates/pav_core/tests/games.rs` to `tests/<key>.rs`: the bot wins on\n   ten seeds, a careless player loses, and snapshots, rewind and replays are exact.\n\n## The level: a room file\nA level is a room file (`rooms/_template.toml` documents every field; the pavilion's\n`rooms/*.toml` are working examples). Rooms built this way stand alone, with no wing or\ncorridor. In a room file:\n- `[layout]`: an ASCII map per floor (`[[layout.layer]]`, 1 cell = 1 m; column = x east,\n  row = z south) and a `legend` per character: floor and wall `blocks` (heights, colours,\n  `destructible`, `crumble`, `ghost`), ladders, `prop`s, `label`s, `grass`, trigger `zone`s\n  (`kill`, `water`, `checkpoint`, `conveyor`, `bounce`, `pad`, `camera`, course\n  `start`/`gate`/`finish`) and `marker = \"<name>\"`.\n- **Markers** are your own pieces: `crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\")` lists every\n  marked cell (world positions, map order). `marker = \"player\"` is the start.\n- `[[npc]]`: characters (`kind = biped | spider | lizard | beetle | blob | quadruped`, `look` colours and\n  proportions, `ai = idle | wander | patrol | circle | follow | guard`). Your game can drive\n  them instead (the starter's slimes).\n- `[[object]]`: library props (`asset = \"LIB/core/<name>\"`; `pav assets find=barrel` searches\n  the store, `assets shelf=prop` lists the best), physics objects, `[[joint]]`s and `[[chain]]`s.\n- `[camera]` (`tilt`, `distance`, `yaw`, `ortho`), `[view]` (light, sky, fog, bloom, styles,\n  filters: `pav params prefix=view` lists them), `[params]` (movement and physics while in\n  the level: `pav params`).\n\n- `[game]`: your rules' numbers, free-form; your `build` reads them (`def.game`).\n- `entrance` is required by the format; with a `player` marker a level doesn't use it.\n\nRead the level with `games::level_text(KEY, include_str!(...))`: the file on disk when running\nfrom the repository, else the copy built in (tests, the browser, an executable). The camera\nfollows the player; to see a whole level from above, `camera preset=top` then\n`set path=camera.distance value=30` (raise it for bigger levels) and `capture`.\n\n## The hook: `pav_core::games::Game`\n```rust\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct MyGame { /* the rules' state: score, timers, entity ids, outcome */ }\n\nimpl Game for MyGame {\n    const KEY: &'static str = \"mygame\";\n    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>)\n        -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame>;          // before movement: inputs for characters you drive\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>); // after physics: rules\n    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String>;        // text lines, top left; `play` returns them\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value; // your state for tools and tests\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome;                   // Playing | Won | Lost\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame;         // what a good player would press now\n}\npub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()>; // the scene; sets sim.state.play\n```\nEvery method has a default. `build` gets the rest of a scene name `mygame/<rest>` (a level\nnumber, say). Each tick runs: behaviours, your `pre`, characters move (your inputs replace\ntheir own), physics, shots move and hit, your `post`. While `pre`/`post` run, your game is out\nof `sim.state.play`: use `self` for your state and `sim` for the world. In `post`, `events`\nalready holds this tick's events: read them to react (`Hit { target, .. }` names the\ncharacter a shot struck: it is stunned, knocked back and safe from shots for 0.6 s), and push\nyour own. From outside, `sim.play()?.get::<MyGame>()` reads your game; in a test,\n`sim.state.play.as_mut().unwrap().get_mut::<MyGame>().unwrap()` changes it.\n\n## Building blocks\n| You need | Use |\n|---|---|\n| The player | `sim.state.player`; it walks by the keys' `InputFrame` (`move_dir`, `buttons::JUMP`, `PRIMARY`...); `sim.feet(id)` is where a character stands (`pos` is its centre) |\n| Characters | `[[npc]]` in the level, or `sim.spawn_npc(name, feet, facing, PuppetDef::preset(BodyPlan::Blob), None, None)`; drive them from `pre` |\n| Pickups, hazards, scenery | `sim.spawn(Spawn::new(\"name\", pos).visual(Visual::new(shape, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"))).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed }))`; `sim.despawn(id)`; `sim.set_position(id, feet)` |\n| Moving platforms, emitters, spinners | `Behavior::Move`, `Rotate`, `Emitter`, `Spawner` (`entity.rs`), or `[[object]]` in the level |\n| Shots | `sim.state.projectiles.spawn(Projectile { pos, vel, radius, life, color, knockback, gravity, owner, team, damage })`: plain structs, stopped by walls; `Team::Enemy` shots hit characters (a `Hit` event), `Team::Player` shots hit things with `health` |\n| Pathfinding | `nav::NavGrid::for_room(sim)` (the level's walkable grid); `grid.path(from, to)`; `grid.flow(goal, reach)` steers many chasers at once; `grid.walkable(p)` |\n| Randomness | `sim.state.rng` only (`range`, `below`, `f32`): seeded, so a seed always plays the same |\n| Messages | `sim.say(\"text\")`: centre top for a few seconds |\n| Sound and effects | push `SimEvent`s from `pre`/`post`: `Coin`, `Pickup`, `Hit`, `Explosion`, `Shot`, `Jump`, `Land`... The renderer and the synth play them |\n| Tuning | your numbers: the level's `[game]`; the engine's: `pav params`, `pav set path=movement.speed value=4.5` (movement, physics, camera, view), and a level's `[params]` set them while in it |\n| Looks | the level's `[view]`; `pav look` lists looks and filters; `rooms/mix_match.toml` mixes styles per part |\n| Animation | characters animate procedurally; motion clips: `pav assets shelf=clip`, then `clips = [\"SET/Clip\"]` on an `[[npc]]` |\n\n## Rules that keep it working\n- **Same inputs and seed, same game.** Random numbers come from `sim.state.rng`; never read the\n  clock; never let `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (`BTreeMap`, or sort).\n- **All game state lives in your struct or in entities**, and your struct is `Clone` + serde\n  (snapshots are clones; files and the replay hash are its CBOR). A cache that follows from\n  the level (a nav grid) is `#[serde(skip)]` and rebuilt when missing.\n- **`pav_core` has no window, GPU or audio code**, and game code must run in the browser: no\n  threads, blocking calls or file access (embed data with `include_str!`).\n- **Check with the tools, not by reading engine code.** If a tool or error message didn't tell\n  you what you needed, that is engine feedback.\n\n## Check your work (no screen needed)\n| Question | Command (`pav <tool>` or a `pav repl` line) |\n|---|---|\n| Can it be won? Lost? | `play bot=true seeds=10` (wins, seconds, the state of every run not won) |\n| What's on screen? | `capture width=640 height=360 out=out/a.png`; `filmstrip` for motion in one image; to watch the bot, repeat `play bot=true ticks=30` and `capture` in a `pav repl` |\n| Does a rule fire? | `input move=[1,0] ticks=30`, then `play` (HUD, outcome, `status`) |\n| Where is everything? | `entities`, `player`, `status` (with the state hash) |\n| Same every time? | `record_save path=out/r.json`, then `replay path=out/r.json` (checks the hash); `rewind`, `snapshot_save`/`snapshot_load` |\n| Is the level valid? | `room_check path=crates/pav_core/src/games/<key>.toml` |\n| Does it run fast? | `bench scene=<key> ticks=1200` |\n| Does it still pass? | `cargo test -p pav_core --test <key>`, `cargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo fmt --all` |\n\n## Ship it\n- **Window:** `cargo run -p pav_app -- --scene <key>` (Linux; `xvfb-run -a` without a display).\n  F5 restarts the game (Esc opens the menu).\n- **Windows `.exe`:** `scripts/build-windows.sh` (after `scripts/setup-linux.sh`).\n- **Browser, one file:** `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene <key> --title \"My Game\"\n  --out out/mygame.html`: about 10 MB, runs from `file://` in Chrome or Edge (WebGPU).\n\n## Limits today\n- HUD: text lines only (captures draw them; `ui` reads them). Panels, bars or menus mean egui\n  code in `pav_ui` (draw it from `headless.rs` too, so the tools see it).\n- Drawing from game state without an entity (`pav_view::assets::emit_asset`) and new sounds\n  (`pav_audio`, a closed list) mean code outside `pav_core`.\n- Your game compiles into the engine crate (`pav_core/src/games/`), beside Shardfall.\n- The camera follows the player with the level's `[camera]` settings; there is no\n  per-game camera code. Keep levels about 20 m across, or raise `[camera] distance`.\n- The window's controls card lists the engine demo's keys (bombs, vehicles), not your game's.\n\n## Report back\nWhen you hand the game back, add a short **engine feedback** section, written for an engine\nagent who has never seen your work: what you used, the engine source you had to read and\nwhy, what you wrote that the engine should have had (with the file), wrong or missing docs,\nand the one change that would have helped most. The maintainers log it in\n`docs/FEEDBACK.md`, and what keeps coming back becomes engine work.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Grw1qAAxAfscomnFfERNdb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj92KUTyTYZ6AFdv5v4b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WcjhNLKXVfmqXw56nQT4bo","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"gh api repos/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/contents/lib/hub/README.md -H \"Accept: application/vnd.github.raw\" 2>&1","description":"Fetch the AI Hub library README"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9403,"cache_read_input_tokens":25479,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9403},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33b9a197-b2fe-42e5-9a43-b32ae2dde858","uuid":"c35e9e0b-63bb-4e58-ac3a-a51766a7e68f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:07.139Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj92KD6UhCiR1ReJYfkU","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01WcjhNLKXVfmqXw56nQT4bo":{"command":"gh api repos/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/contents/lib/hub/README.md -H \"Accept: application/vnd.github.raw\" 2>&1","description":"Fetch the AI Hub library README"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WcjhNLKXVfmqXw56nQT4bo","type":"tool_result","content":"# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n\n---\n\n## Modes\n\nA game declares one mode.\n\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n|---|---|---|\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n  world the host sends them.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n\n---\n\n## A round\n\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\n   (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n\n### The start\n\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n`start`.\n\n### Reporting\n\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n  by the host only:\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n    placed last.\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n\n### Time-up and the end\n\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n  `finish()` by then is void.\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n  game, then removes it.\n\n| `end` reason | |\n|---|---|\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n\n### Leaving\n\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\nresults:\n\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n\n---\n\n## Sending data between copies\n\n### Parallel\n\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n\n### Ghost\n\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n\n### Shared world\n\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n  every copy.\n\n### `send()` and `message`\n\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n  client drops the rest.\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n\n---\n\n## Rules\n\nIn a session:\n\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n  names. Names come from the hub.\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n  is held.\n\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\none, the game is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Test it\n\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\nto the game:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n  player of its own;\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n  told the round ended;\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\n\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\nruns there.\n\n---\n\n## Reference\n\n### Tags\n\n| Tag | |\n|---|---|\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n\n### API\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\nread them.\n\n| Event | Arguments |\n|---|---|\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n| `message` | `data, from` |\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\n\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\n\n### Versions\n\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\nprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33b9a197-b2fe-42e5-9a43-b32ae2dde858","uuid":"1b0c55f1-eebc-4f15-9d6e-b85db5b14da1","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:07.619Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n\n---\n\n## Modes\n\nA game declares one mode.\n\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n|---|---|---|\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n  world the host sends them.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n\n---\n\n## A round\n\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\n   (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n\n### The start\n\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n`start`.\n\n### Reporting\n\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n  by the host only:\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n    placed last.\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n\n### Time-up and the end\n\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n  `finish()` by then is void.\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n  game, then removes it.\n\n| `end` reason | |\n|---|---|\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n\n### Leaving\n\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\nresults:\n\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n\n---\n\n## Sending data between copies\n\n### Parallel\n\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n\n### Ghost\n\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n\n### Shared world\n\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n  every copy.\n\n### `send()` and `message`\n\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n  client drops the rest.\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n\n---\n\n## Rules\n\nIn a session:\n\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n  names. Names come from the hub.\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n  is held.\n\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\none, the game is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Test it\n\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\nto the game:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n  player of its own;\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n  told the round ended;\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\n\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\nruns there.\n\n---\n\n## Reference\n\n### Tags\n\n| Tag | |\n|---|---|\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n\n### API\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\nread them.\n\n| Event | Arguments |\n|---|---|\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n| `message` | `data, from` |\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\n\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\n\n### Versions\n\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\nprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WcjhNLKXVfmqXw56nQT4bo","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
