{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj96NzQc31W4h1iMzXhh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LGBe5mAhhXgxPzy3TG4rMY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/.scout/shardfall/docs/GAME.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5883,"cache_read_input_tokens":59538,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5883},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33b9a197-b2fe-42e5-9a43-b32ae2dde858","uuid":"803a171e-6b63-4d2d-a60c-3c8198369394","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:01.757Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj96Nk25ZgsUr2Lej92v","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01LGBe5mAhhXgxPzy3TG4rMY":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/.scout/shardfall/docs/GAME.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LGBe5mAhhXgxPzy3TG4rMY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Shardfall — the showcase game\n2\t\n3\tA fast hack-and-slash built on the Pavilion engine. Run-based: from the town you take a\n4\tportal into a level, clear it, find the exit, and go deeper. Twelve designed levels each\n5\tintroduce one mechanic (and are named after it); after that the Depths generate levels forever\n6\tby combining mechanics, monster archetypes, bosses and colour palettes.\n7\t\n8\tIt is also the reference for agents building games on this engine: every system is data plus\n9\tsmall generators, and every system has an agent tool to build and inspect it.\n10\t\n11\t## Pillars\n12\t1. **Fast and fluid.** Move with WASD, aim with the mouse, attack on press, cancel\n13\t   recovery into a dodge, and get hit-stop, shake, flash and knockback on every hit.\n14\t2. **Deep builds.** A large passive tree (hundreds of nodes, keystones, skill masteries and an\n15\t   endless repeatable outer ring), 16 active skills, and items with rolled affixes and uniques\n16\t   whose powers change how skills behave.\n17\t3. **Infinite modularity.** Monsters are genomes (body plan + parts + palette + archetype\n18\t   brain + element + affixes). Bosses are genomes plus phase scripts. Items are base + affixes\n19\t   scaled by item level. Levels are a layout grammar (rooms, corridors, arenas) plus a theme\n20\t   plus mechanics. Each part is authored data first, then rolled procedurally from the same\n21\t   vocabulary.\n22\t4. **Engine showcase.** It uses procedural rigs and animation, dynamic lights (spells, braziers,\n23\t   darkness), particles, bloom, distortion, physics (loot bounces, barrels roll, debris),\n24\t   rewind, the room/layout builder, synth audio, the agent tools and the live bridge.\n25\t5. **Bypassable mechanics.** A casual player can ignore any level mechanic and still clear the\n26\t   level. Speedrunners and farmers can exploit it: kegs chain through packs, currents carry you\n27\t   past rooms, portals skip routes, and darkness doubles drops.\n28\t\n29\t## Architecture\n30\t- `pav_core::arpg` holds the game rules, inside the simulation (`SimState::game`), so\n31\t  snapshots, rewind, replays, the agent tools and the live bridge all work on the game.\n32\t  - `stats` (stat ids, stat blocks, aggregation)\n33\t  - `combat` (actors, damage, ailments, hit-stop)\n34\t  - `skills` (active skills as data and behaviours)\n35\t  - `hero` (profile: level, XP, gold, items, tree, skill bar; saved as JSON)\n36\t  - `items` and `loot` (bases, affixes, uniques, drops)\n37\t  - `tree` (passive tree data and layout)\n38\t  - `genome` (monster generator, parts, palettes, names), `brain` (archetypes), `boss`\n39\t  - `levelgen` (layout grammar, themes, endless levels), `mechanics` (the twelve), `town`\n40\t- `game/` (repo root) holds the data: skills, item bases and affixes, uniques, tree clusters,\n41\t  monster families, palettes, themes, the designed levels and the level dressing sets. It is\n42\t  embedded in the executable.\n43\t- `pav_view::arpg` draws the game: weapons in hand, skill effects, telegraphs, ground loot\n44\t  with rarity beams, damage numbers and monster auras.\n45\t- `pav_app::arpg_ui` is the game UI: life and mana orbs, skill bar, XP, inventory, tooltips,\n46\t  passive tree, vendor, stash, map, death screen, waypoints and the difficulty sliders.\n47\t- `pav_tools` adds the game tools: `hero`, `give`, `monster`, `turntable`, `animsheet`,\n48\t  `loot_roll`, `tree_info`, `levelgen`, `levelmap`, `duel` and `autoplay`.\n49\t\n50\t## Milestones\n51\t- **G1 Combat core:** arena, hero combo, dodge and first skills, three monster archetypes with\n52\t  telegraphs, damage, death, XP and gold, HUD, hit feel, difficulty sliders.\n53\t- **G2 Loot:** items, affixes, rarity, drops, pickup, inventory and equipment, tooltips, stats\n54\t  from gear, vendor and stash, weapon visuals. *(done: see \"Items\" below)*\n55\t- **G3 Builds:** passive tree (data + layout + UI), all 16 skills, masteries, keystones,\n56\t  respec. *(done: see \"Builds\" below)*\n57\t- **G4 Monsters:** genome, parts rendering, palettes, archetype brains, monster affixes, boss\n58\t  phases, creature lab. *(done: see \"Monsters\" below)*\n59\t- **G5 World:** town hub with animated NPCs, layout generator and themes, twelve mechanics,\n60\t  designed levels 1-12, endless Depths, waypoints, saving. *(done: see \"World\" below)*\n61\t- **G6 Showcase:** juice pass, the agent tools, bot balance pass, browser build, docs and the\n62\t  final build. *(done)*\n63\t\n64\t## Items (G2)\n65\t- **Bases** (`game/items.toml`, 114): ten weapon kinds and nine slots, six tiers each by item\n66\t  level (1, 10, 22, 38, 55, 75). Numbers grow with item level by `base_scale` (x45 at 75, and on\n67\t  forever), so a tier is a look and a bigger implicit, never a dead end.\n68\t- **Affixes** (`game/affixes.toml`, 74): prefix/suffix, slot lists (slot names, weapon kinds or\n69\t  `armor`), tiers `[ilvl, min, max]`; past the last tier values grow by `grow` per level\n70\t  (percent stats cap with `grow = 0`). `local` affixes change the item itself (weapon damage,\n71\t  attack speed, crit; armour). Magic: 1-2 affixes, rare: 3-6 (max 3 per side).\n72\t- **Uniques** (`game/uniques.toml`, 19): fixed stats scaled to the drop level and a **power**.\n73\t  Powers (`arpg/powers.rs`, 16 kinds: corpse burst, frost crits, blood magic, execute, fire\n74\t  trail, orbiting blades, echo strike, dodge reset, storm call, block nova, ignite aura, stand\n75\t  firm, meteor slam, frenzy, mana shield, quickening) belong to actors, not items, so passive\n76\t  keystones (G3) and monster affixes (G4) reuse them. Power hits use internal `power_*` skills\n77\t  in skills.toml.\n78\t- **Drops**: item chance per kill by monster rarity (bosses shower), rarity weights 70/24/5.5/0.5\n79\t  shifted by item rarity and monster rank, slot shares fixed regardless of base counts. Items\n80\t  pop out and bounce (simple ballistic flight, ground found by ray), gold piles fly to the hero.\n81\t  Auto-pickup by rarity filter; labels on the ground are clickable.\n82\t- **Commands**: every menu action is a `GameCmd` carried in `InputFrame::cmd` (queued one per\n83\t  tick by the app), so trading and equipping record, replay and rewind exactly.\n84\t- **Places**: `Place::Town` (Emberwatch: Hilda the smith sells and buys, the stash, a portal)\n85\t  and `Place::Arena` (the Proving Grounds). Travel rebuilds the scene inside the same tick and\n86\t  random stream, carrying the `Game` (hero, bags, settings).\n87\t- **Looks**: the hero's puppet wears the gear: weapon kind/colour/glow, shield or focus, helmet\n88\t  shapes (cap, helm, great helm, crown, horned, halo), shoulder plates, cape, gloves, buckle.\n89\t\n90\t## Builds (G3)\n91\t- **Skills**: 16 for the hero, from quick attacks to channelled spins, war cries, meteors and\n92\t  blizzards; every behaviour is data (`behavior` in skills.toml), and monsters can use any skill.\n93\t- **Tweaks** change one skill (more projectiles, bigger area, an element...). They come from the\n94\t  tree's skill branches today and can come from items and monster affixes next.\n95\t- **Passive tree**: six sectors - Might, Fury, Precision, Arcana, Elements, Bulwark - each a\n96\t  road with notables, three wheels around masteries (pick one of four options), up to three\n97\t  skill branches and a keystone; bridges between neighbours carry hybrid stats and six more\n98\t  keystones (Echoing Blades, Wind Dancer, Glass Edge, Avatar of Storm, Pyre, Avatar of Flame).\n99\t  Beyond: the Astral rings, endless, stronger ring by ring. One point per level; refund and\n100\t  respec cost gold.\n101\t\n102\t## Characters\n103\tThree playable characters (`game/heroes.toml`), each a hero of their own (level, gear, passives,\n104\twaypoints) waiting in the Hall of Heroes while another is played; the stash is shared. Skills\n105\tare shared too: a character changes how a skill looks, not what it does.\n106\t- **The Wanderer, the Sellsword**: the engine's procedural animation; a sword. Who a new game\n107\t  starts as.\n108\t- **Kestrel the Brawler**: compact, bare-chested, mohawk; hooks. Captured boxing and karate (a\n109\t  boxer's guard, a jab-cross-roundhouse combo, a chi blast, a ground pound), fist and kick moves\n110\t  (sweep, axe kick, flying kick, uppercut), a captured roll for a dodge.\n111\t- **Ysolde the Stormcaller**: tall, hooded, robed, motes of light circling; a staff that glows.\n112\t  Captured spellwork (a spell held ready, fireball, two-handed blast, rising to call a meteor),\n113\t  staff strikes (thrust, backhand, rising cut), a forward glide for a dodge.\n114\t\n115\t## Monsters (G4)\n116\t- **Genome**: body plan × proportions × parts × palette (element) × archetype (brain, skill\n117\t  pools, stat shape) → a named creature, the same from the same seed. Five body plans, ten\n118\t  part kinds that fit any plan, five elements, nine archetypes (brute, stalker, spitter,\n119\t  charger, caster, swarm, bomber, summoner, tank).\n120\t- **Affixes** make magic and rare monsters (Fast, Molten, Frost-Touched, Multishot,\n121\t  Teleporting, Warlord, Brood, Enraging...); rares get names of their own.\n122\t- **Bosses** grow from genome seeds with forced looks and scripted phases; beyond the five\n123\t  designed ones they are generated (summon a brood, change tactics, enrage).\n124\t- **The Menagerie** shows twenty creatures at a time with their genome cards; let any of them\n125\t  out to fight it, or grow a new set.\n126\t\n127\t## World (G5)\n128\t- **Emberwatch** (the town): Hilda the smith (vendor), Odo the gambler (a sealed box per slot:\n129\t  magic 60%, rare 22%, unique 3%), Mother Wren the alchemist (up to 6 potions, stronger brews),\n130\t  Captain Brannoc by the portal, villagers on their rounds and Biscuit the dog. The portal\n131\t  lists every depth you've reached. The Hall of Heroes (south side) has a pedestal for every\n132\t  playable character, each performing a showreel; use one to play as them.\n133\t- **The descent**: each level is rooms and corridors generated from a seed, painted by a theme,\n134\t  with one signature mechanic and earlier ones mixed in. Find the way down; on boss levels it\n135\t  is sealed until the boss falls (and the first time pays a passive point).\n136\t- **Dressing**: each room gets 3-6 library props (embedded `LIB/core` assets, in the theme's\n137\t  `$wall`, `$pillar`, `$accent` and `$light` colours) from the sets its theme names in `props`.\n138\t  The sets are `game/props.toml`. Dressing runs last, from its own random stream, so editing it\n139\t  never moves a mechanic or a monster. To re-dress a theme, add a set and name it in the\n140\t  theme's `props` (themes.toml), e.g. `props = [\"ruins\", \"rocks\"]`:\n141\t  ```toml\n142\t  [ruins]                                    # game/props.toml\n143\t  place = \"wall\"                             # wall, corner, floor or wall_mount\n144\t  items = [{ asset = \"LIB/core/column_broken\", w = 2 }, { asset = \"LIB/core/urn\" }]\n145\t  ```\n146\t  `assets find=` searches what there is; `levelmap depth=N` shows where props stand (grey boxes).\n147\t\n148\t| # | Level | Mechanic | Also | Boss |\n149\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n150\t| 1 | The Shrines | shrines: walk through for a boon | | |\n151\t| 2 | The Powder Keg | kegs: hit one near a pack | | |\n152\t| 3 | The Gauntlet | spike plates on a beat | shrines | |\n153\t| 4 | The Rift Gates | paired gates, shortcuts | kegs | The Hollow King |\n154\t| 5 | The Windways | wind carries everyone | spikes | |\n155\t| 6 | The Totem Fields | totems ward monsters | shrines, kegs | |\n156\t| 7 | The Molten Floor | lava burns whoever stands in it | wind | Cinder Wyrm |\n157\t| 8 | The Frozen Lake | ice: slide; frozen foes shatter | totems, spikes | |\n158\t| 9 | The Crumbling Halls | the floor falls behind you | gates, kegs | |\n159\t| 10 | The Lightless Deep | darkness; light the wells | shrines, lava | Mother of Swarms |\n160\t| 11 | The Cursed Vaults | chests call keepers, then pay | wind, totems | |\n161\t| 12 | The Time Rift | bubbles of slow time | crumbling, ice | Frostbound Colossus |\n162\t\n163\t- **The Depths** (13 onward, forever): a theme turned around the colour wheel and lit by\n164\t  another, two to four mechanics at once, monsters grown with the depth's favoured\n165\t  archetypes, a boss every third depth. \"The Howling Molten Caverns\" is always Depth N; its\n166\t  rooms are new every visit.\n167\t- **Mechanics are optional**: every level can be cleared by fighting. Exploits: chain kegs\n168\t  through packs, ride the wind, kite monsters into lava or over spikes, freeze them on ice and\n169\t  shatter them, let them fall through the floor you broke, light wells mid-fight, fight inside\n170\t  a time bubble, take the gate shortcut straight to the exit.\n171\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33b9a197-b2fe-42e5-9a43-b32ae2dde858","uuid":"ae28036a-6165-496c-8d0c-d3fb44c006eb","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:01.769Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/.scout/shardfall/docs/GAME.md","content":"# Shardfall — the showcase game\n\nA fast hack-and-slash built on the Pavilion engine. Run-based: from the town you take a\nportal into a level, clear it, find the exit, and go deeper. Twelve designed levels each\nintroduce one mechanic (and are named after it); after that the Depths generate levels forever\nby combining mechanics, monster archetypes, bosses and colour palettes.\n\nIt is also the reference for agents building games on this engine: every system is data plus\nsmall generators, and every system has an agent tool to build and inspect it.\n\n## Pillars\n1. **Fast and fluid.** Move with WASD, aim with the mouse, attack on press, cancel\n   recovery into a dodge, and get hit-stop, shake, flash and knockback on every hit.\n2. **Deep builds.** A large passive tree (hundreds of nodes, keystones, skill masteries and an\n   endless repeatable outer ring), 16 active skills, and items with rolled affixes and uniques\n   whose powers change how skills behave.\n3. **Infinite modularity.** Monsters are genomes (body plan + parts + palette + archetype\n   brain + element + affixes). Bosses are genomes plus phase scripts. Items are base + affixes\n   scaled by item level. Levels are a layout grammar (rooms, corridors, arenas) plus a theme\n   plus mechanics. Each part is authored data first, then rolled procedurally from the same\n   vocabulary.\n4. **Engine showcase.** It uses procedural rigs and animation, dynamic lights (spells, braziers,\n   darkness), particles, bloom, distortion, physics (loot bounces, barrels roll, debris),\n   rewind, the room/layout builder, synth audio, the agent tools and the live bridge.\n5. **Bypassable mechanics.** A casual player can ignore any level mechanic and still clear the\n   level. Speedrunners and farmers can exploit it: kegs chain through packs, currents carry you\n   past rooms, portals skip routes, and darkness doubles drops.\n\n## Architecture\n- `pav_core::arpg` holds the game rules, inside the simulation (`SimState::game`), so\n  snapshots, rewind, replays, the agent tools and the live bridge all work on the game.\n  - `stats` (stat ids, stat blocks, aggregation)\n  - `combat` (actors, damage, ailments, hit-stop)\n  - `skills` (active skills as data and behaviours)\n  - `hero` (profile: level, XP, gold, items, tree, skill bar; saved as JSON)\n  - `items` and `loot` (bases, affixes, uniques, drops)\n  - `tree` (passive tree data and layout)\n  - `genome` (monster generator, parts, palettes, names), `brain` (archetypes), `boss`\n  - `levelgen` (layout grammar, themes, endless levels), `mechanics` (the twelve), `town`\n- `game/` (repo root) holds the data: skills, item bases and affixes, uniques, tree clusters,\n  monster families, palettes, themes, the designed levels and the level dressing sets. It is\n  embedded in the executable.\n- `pav_view::arpg` draws the game: weapons in hand, skill effects, telegraphs, ground loot\n  with rarity beams, damage numbers and monster auras.\n- `pav_app::arpg_ui` is the game UI: life and mana orbs, skill bar, XP, inventory, tooltips,\n  passive tree, vendor, stash, map, death screen, waypoints and the difficulty sliders.\n- `pav_tools` adds the game tools: `hero`, `give`, `monster`, `turntable`, `animsheet`,\n  `loot_roll`, `tree_info`, `levelgen`, `levelmap`, `duel` and `autoplay`.\n\n## Milestones\n- **G1 Combat core:** arena, hero combo, dodge and first skills, three monster archetypes with\n  telegraphs, damage, death, XP and gold, HUD, hit feel, difficulty sliders.\n- **G2 Loot:** items, affixes, rarity, drops, pickup, inventory and equipment, tooltips, stats\n  from gear, vendor and stash, weapon visuals. *(done: see \"Items\" below)*\n- **G3 Builds:** passive tree (data + layout + UI), all 16 skills, masteries, keystones,\n  respec. *(done: see \"Builds\" below)*\n- **G4 Monsters:** genome, parts rendering, palettes, archetype brains, monster affixes, boss\n  phases, creature lab. *(done: see \"Monsters\" below)*\n- **G5 World:** town hub with animated NPCs, layout generator and themes, twelve mechanics,\n  designed levels 1-12, endless Depths, waypoints, saving. *(done: see \"World\" below)*\n- **G6 Showcase:** juice pass, the agent tools, bot balance pass, browser build, docs and the\n  final build. *(done)*\n\n## Items (G2)\n- **Bases** (`game/items.toml`, 114): ten weapon kinds and nine slots, six tiers each by item\n  level (1, 10, 22, 38, 55, 75). Numbers grow with item level by `base_scale` (x45 at 75, and on\n  forever), so a tier is a look and a bigger implicit, never a dead end.\n- **Affixes** (`game/affixes.toml`, 74): prefix/suffix, slot lists (slot names, weapon kinds or\n  `armor`), tiers `[ilvl, min, max]`; past the last tier values grow by `grow` per level\n  (percent stats cap with `grow = 0`). `local` affixes change the item itself (weapon damage,\n  attack speed, crit; armour). Magic: 1-2 affixes, rare: 3-6 (max 3 per side).\n- **Uniques** (`game/uniques.toml`, 19): fixed stats scaled to the drop level and a **power**.\n  Powers (`arpg/powers.rs`, 16 kinds: corpse burst, frost crits, blood magic, execute, fire\n  trail, orbiting blades, echo strike, dodge reset, storm call, block nova, ignite aura, stand\n  firm, meteor slam, frenzy, mana shield, quickening) belong to actors, not items, so passive\n  keystones (G3) and monster affixes (G4) reuse them. Power hits use internal `power_*` skills\n  in skills.toml.\n- **Drops**: item chance per kill by monster rarity (bosses shower), rarity weights 70/24/5.5/0.5\n  shifted by item rarity and monster rank, slot shares fixed regardless of base counts. Items\n  pop out and bounce (simple ballistic flight, ground found by ray), gold piles fly to the hero.\n  Auto-pickup by rarity filter; labels on the ground are clickable.\n- **Commands**: every menu action is a `GameCmd` carried in `InputFrame::cmd` (queued one per\n  tick by the app), so trading and equipping record, replay and rewind exactly.\n- **Places**: `Place::Town` (Emberwatch: Hilda the smith sells and buys, the stash, a portal)\n  and `Place::Arena` (the Proving Grounds). Travel rebuilds the scene inside the same tick and\n  random stream, carrying the `Game` (hero, bags, settings).\n- **Looks**: the hero's puppet wears the gear: weapon kind/colour/glow, shield or focus, helmet\n  shapes (cap, helm, great helm, crown, horned, halo), shoulder plates, cape, gloves, buckle.\n\n## Builds (G3)\n- **Skills**: 16 for the hero, from quick attacks to channelled spins, war cries, meteors and\n  blizzards; every behaviour is data (`behavior` in skills.toml), and monsters can use any skill.\n- **Tweaks** change one skill (more projectiles, bigger area, an element...). They come from the\n  tree's skill branches today and can come from items and monster affixes next.\n- **Passive tree**: six sectors - Might, Fury, Precision, Arcana, Elements, Bulwark - each a\n  road with notables, three wheels around masteries (pick one of four options), up to three\n  skill branches and a keystone; bridges between neighbours carry hybrid stats and six more\n  keystones (Echoing Blades, Wind Dancer, Glass Edge, Avatar of Storm, Pyre, Avatar of Flame).\n  Beyond: the Astral rings, endless, stronger ring by ring. One point per level; refund and\n  respec cost gold.\n\n## Characters\nThree playable characters (`game/heroes.toml`), each a hero of their own (level, gear, passives,\nwaypoints) waiting in the Hall of Heroes while another is played; the stash is shared. Skills\nare shared too: a character changes how a skill looks, not what it does.\n- **The Wanderer, the Sellsword**: the engine's procedural animation; a sword. Who a new game\n  starts as.\n- **Kestrel the Brawler**: compact, bare-chested, mohawk; hooks. Captured boxing and karate (a\n  boxer's guard, a jab-cross-roundhouse combo, a chi blast, a ground pound), fist and kick moves\n  (sweep, axe kick, flying kick, uppercut), a captured roll for a dodge.\n- **Ysolde the Stormcaller**: tall, hooded, robed, motes of light circling; a staff that glows.\n  Captured spellwork (a spell held ready, fireball, two-handed blast, rising to call a meteor),\n  staff strikes (thrust, backhand, rising cut), a forward glide for a dodge.\n\n## Monsters (G4)\n- **Genome**: body plan × proportions × parts × palette (element) × archetype (brain, skill\n  pools, stat shape) → a named creature, the same from the same seed. Five body plans, ten\n  part kinds that fit any plan, five elements, nine archetypes (brute, stalker, spitter,\n  charger, caster, swarm, bomber, summoner, tank).\n- **Affixes** make magic and rare monsters (Fast, Molten, Frost-Touched, Multishot,\n  Teleporting, Warlord, Brood, Enraging...); rares get names of their own.\n- **Bosses** grow from genome seeds with forced looks and scripted phases; beyond the five\n  designed ones they are generated (summon a brood, change tactics, enrage).\n- **The Menagerie** shows twenty creatures at a time with their genome cards; let any of them\n  out to fight it, or grow a new set.\n\n## World (G5)\n- **Emberwatch** (the town): Hilda the smith (vendor), Odo the gambler (a sealed box per slot:\n  magic 60%, rare 22%, unique 3%), Mother Wren the alchemist (up to 6 potions, stronger brews),\n  Captain Brannoc by the portal, villagers on their rounds and Biscuit the dog. The portal\n  lists every depth you've reached. The Hall of Heroes (south side) has a pedestal for every\n  playable character, each performing a showreel; use one to play as them.\n- **The descent**: each level is rooms and corridors generated from a seed, painted by a theme,\n  with one signature mechanic and earlier ones mixed in. Find the way down; on boss levels it\n  is sealed until the boss falls (and the first time pays a passive point).\n- **Dressing**: each room gets 3-6 library props (embedded `LIB/core` assets, in the theme's\n  `$wall`, `$pillar`, `$accent` and `$light` colours) from the sets its theme names in `props`.\n  The sets are `game/props.toml`. Dressing runs last, from its own random stream, so editing it\n  never moves a mechanic or a monster. To re-dress a theme, add a set and name it in the\n  theme's `props` (themes.toml), e.g. `props = [\"ruins\", \"rocks\"]`:\n  ```toml\n  [ruins]                                    # game/props.toml\n  place = \"wall\"                             # wall, corner, floor or wall_mount\n  items = [{ asset = \"LIB/core/column_broken\", w = 2 }, { asset = \"LIB/core/urn\" }]\n  ```\n  `assets find=` searches what there is; `levelmap depth=N` shows where props stand (grey boxes).\n\n| # | Level | Mechanic | Also | Boss |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | The Shrines | shrines: walk through for a boon | | |\n| 2 | The Powder Keg | kegs: hit one near a pack | | |\n| 3 | The Gauntlet | spike plates on a beat | shrines | |\n| 4 | The Rift Gates | paired gates, shortcuts | kegs | The Hollow King |\n| 5 | The Windways | wind carries everyone | spikes | |\n| 6 | The Totem Fields | totems ward monsters | shrines, kegs | |\n| 7 | The Molten Floor | lava burns whoever stands in it | wind | Cinder Wyrm |\n| 8 | The Frozen Lake | ice: slide; frozen foes shatter | totems, spikes | |\n| 9 | The Crumbling Halls | the floor falls behind you | gates, kegs | |\n| 10 | The Lightless Deep | darkness; light the wells | shrines, lava | Mother of Swarms |\n| 11 | The Cursed Vaults | chests call keepers, then pay | wind, totems | |\n| 12 | The Time Rift | bubbles of slow time | crumbling, ice | Frostbound Colossus |\n\n- **The Depths** (13 onward, forever): a theme turned around the colour wheel and lit by\n  another, two to four mechanics at once, monsters grown with the depth's favoured\n  archetypes, a boss every third depth. \"The Howling Molten Caverns\" is always Depth N; its\n  rooms are new every visit.\n- **Mechanics are optional**: every level can be cleared by fighting. Exploits: chain kegs\n  through packs, ride the wind, kite monsters into lava or over spikes, freeze them on ice and\n  shatter them, let them fall through the floor you broke, light wells mid-fight, fight inside\n  a time bubble, take the gate shortcut straight to the exit.\n","numLines":171,"startLine":1,"totalLines":171}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01LGBe5mAhhXgxPzy3TG4rMY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj96NzQc31W4h1iMzXhh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014V4sf5DfcrBWkLgY5z97JC","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/.scout/shardfall/docs/FEEDBACK.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5883,"cache_read_input_tokens":59538,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5883},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33b9a197-b2fe-42e5-9a43-b32ae2dde858","uuid":"d1d5dd1d-6b67-4991-8d4b-47cddc3b665f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:02.488Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj96Nk25ZgsUr2Lej92v","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_014V4sf5DfcrBWkLgY5z97JC":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/.scout/shardfall/docs/FEEDBACK.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014V4sf5DfcrBWkLgY5z97JC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Test-game feedback\n2\t\n3\tEngine feedback from test games: a fresh agent builds a vertical slice of a 1985-2015 classic on\n4\tthis engine, then evaluates the engine. Each game is stored as one self-contained HTML file in\n5\t`michaelcrosato/ai-hub` (`games/`, source zip in `sources/`). The maintainer checks each claim\n6\tagainst the code, fixes what blocks the next game, and waits for patterns before larger work:\n7\tfeatures everyone uses, features no one uses, problems that keep coming back. Newest game first\n8\tunder \"Games\"; the tally is updated with every game.\n9\t\n10\t**Prompt.** The user's template, with the game's name, year and platforms as SOURCE: build an\n11\toriginal spiritual successor as one complete, polished level that doubles as a tech demo; use the\n12\tengine, extend it as needed; deliver one self-contained HTML file; then a one-page candid\n13\tevaluation of the engine (used/not used, engine work you wrote ranked by output saved, engine\n14\tchanges, bugs and wrong docs, source you had to read, fidelity limits, testing, what helped and\n15\thurt, missing features by impact, the one change). The questions get adjusted as patterns show. Since 2026-10-16 the agent starts from\n16\t`START_HERE.md`'s \"Make a game\" prompt: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` and the starter game, ending with\n17\tthe guide's engine feedback section, which feeds this log.\n18\t\n19\t**Asset store lines** (the store's S3, 2026-10-15; the user pastes them into the template). Each\n20\tgame's answers count toward the store's critical mass (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md` §13): a need\n21\tis met when the game placed, forked or derived a store entry instead of making it from scratch.\n22\t1. Before you make a prop, material or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the\n23\t   engine's asset store and `assets find=words` searches it (`store/README.md`).\n24\t2. Assets you looked for: one line each, the query and the id you used, or `none`.\n25\t3. Up to 5 things you built that another game could reuse: the file and one line each.\n26\t\n27\tThe user flags the store entries each game used on a per-game review page (`scripts/\n28\treview_page.py` with a `flags` manifest): looks, feels, reads, works or broken (§7).\n29\t\n30\t**Cost** is counted end to end from the agent's transcript (model calls, tokens read, weighted\n31\tcost at cache reads 0.1, cache writes 1.25, visible output 5; hidden reasoning is not recorded).\n32\t\n33\t## Tally (1 game)\n34\t| Asked for | Games | Impact | Status |\n35\t|---|---|---|---|\n36\t| A supported second-game path: a `Game` hook for step, frame, view and HUD instead of editing `sim.rs`, `frame.rs`, `build.rs` and `app.rs` | 1 | high | **done** (2026-10-16): `pav_core::games` (step, HUD text, status, bot), the starter game, `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`; drawing and HUD widgets still mean view or app code |\n37\t| Entities, shots, health and AI that don't require rapier (`health.rs`, `projectile.rs`) | 1 | high | waiting |\n38\t| A single-file web build (gzip + inline) | 1 | high | **done**: `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file` |\n39\t| Primitive-part models drawn per frame from game state (props can't be) | 1 | medium | **done**: `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (plan row 4.1) |\n40\t| Stock HUD widgets (bars, radar, off-screen markers, boss bar, banner) | 1 | medium | waiting |\n41\t| Data-driven sound bank (`pav_audio::sounds_for` is a closed match) | 1 | medium | waiting |\n42\t| Per-scene default camera on `load` | 1 | medium | waiting |\n43\t| HUD visible to headless capture | 1 | medium | **done** (2026-10-19): captures draw the game UI (a hook game's HUD lines too); `ui` reads it as text |\n44\t| Open top-down map data (roads, water, spawns) | 1 | low | waiting |\n45\t| Continuous-play and top-down map captures | 1 | low | waiting |\n46\t\n47\t| Engine part | Used | Skipped |\n48\t|---|---|---|\n49\t| `Sim` (rewind, `state_hash`, snapshot), `pav_render::Scene`, particle presets, `pav_audio` synth, `pav_tools` capture/repl/input, the wasm shell and egui, `Rng`/`InputFrame`/bindings | 1 | 0 |\n50\t| rapier entities, `character.rs`, `nav.rs`, `health.rs`, `projectile.rs`, `vehicle.rs` helicopter, `course.rs`, `zones.rs`, `stealth.rs`, `ai.rs` | 0 | 1 |\n51\t| Rooms/TOML, puppet, moves, clips, the asset and creature studios (props, format 2) | 0 | 1 |\n52\t\n53\t## Games\n54\t\n55\t### Capital Strike, from Jungle Strike (1993; SNES, Genesis) — 2026-10-12\n56\t- **Result:** one mission over Washington. Gun, rockets, homing missiles, flares and a dash;\n57\t  armour, fuel and ammo; winch rescues; a motorcade escort; SAMs, AA guns, tanks, gunships and a\n58\t  three-phase boss; loadouts, difficulties, checkpoints, radar and a procedural soundtrack.\n59\t  `ai-hub/games/Jungle-Strike-Capital-Strike-2026-10-12-model-unknown.html` (9.8 MB); source as a\n60\t  patch series against `6618522` in `ai-hub/sources/`.\n61\t- **Agent:** Sonnet at medium effort, a subagent in its own worktree of this repo.\n62\t- **Cost:** 1.65 h, 170 model calls, 54M tokens read (context grew from 56k to 485k), about 6.5M\n63\t  weighted; 106k tokens of visible output. About 4,270 lines written, 3,965 of them game code\n64\t  inside the engine crates.\n65\t- **Verdict (agent's):** the renderer, wasm shell, audio synth and headless tools carried the\n66\t  visuals and the first wasm build ran; the gameplay half didn't help a non-RPG, non-physics game,\n67\t  so 4,000 lines went into a game layer with no engine facility, and the packaging was all its own.\n68\t- **Checked:** the claims about `vehicle.rs` and `health.rs` hold (rapier-bound; no weapons,\n69\t  damage or fuel), and AGENTS.md now says so. The game didn't touch props, format 2 or the studios:\n70\t  its units are SDF and cube parts drawn in code, because a prop can't be drawn from game state.\n71\t- **Acted on:** the single-file web build (every test game needs one), the AGENTS.md wording, and\n72\t  the asset plan's row 4.1 now includes drawing a library asset from game code. Everything else\n73\t  waits for a second game.\n74\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33b9a197-b2fe-42e5-9a43-b32ae2dde858","uuid":"211ec542-4ac2-4bfa-b418-7c286f23c305","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:02.498Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/.scout/shardfall/docs/FEEDBACK.md","content":"# Test-game feedback\n\nEngine feedback from test games: a fresh agent builds a vertical slice of a 1985-2015 classic on\nthis engine, then evaluates the engine. Each game is stored as one self-contained HTML file in\n`michaelcrosato/ai-hub` (`games/`, source zip in `sources/`). The maintainer checks each claim\nagainst the code, fixes what blocks the next game, and waits for patterns before larger work:\nfeatures everyone uses, features no one uses, problems that keep coming back. Newest game first\nunder \"Games\"; the tally is updated with every game.\n\n**Prompt.** The user's template, with the game's name, year and platforms as SOURCE: build an\noriginal spiritual successor as one complete, polished level that doubles as a tech demo; use the\nengine, extend it as needed; deliver one self-contained HTML file; then a one-page candid\nevaluation of the engine (used/not used, engine work you wrote ranked by output saved, engine\nchanges, bugs and wrong docs, source you had to read, fidelity limits, testing, what helped and\nhurt, missing features by impact, the one change). The questions get adjusted as patterns show. Since 2026-10-16 the agent starts from\n`START_HERE.md`'s \"Make a game\" prompt: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` and the starter game, ending with\nthe guide's engine feedback section, which feeds this log.\n\n**Asset store lines** (the store's S3, 2026-10-15; the user pastes them into the template). Each\ngame's answers count toward the store's critical mass (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md` §13): a need\nis met when the game placed, forked or derived a store entry instead of making it from scratch.\n1. Before you make a prop, material or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the\n   engine's asset store and `assets find=words` searches it (`store/README.md`).\n2. Assets you looked for: one line each, the query and the id you used, or `none`.\n3. Up to 5 things you built that another game could reuse: the file and one line each.\n\nThe user flags the store entries each game used on a per-game review page (`scripts/\nreview_page.py` with a `flags` manifest): looks, feels, reads, works or broken (§7).\n\n**Cost** is counted end to end from the agent's transcript (model calls, tokens read, weighted\ncost at cache reads 0.1, cache writes 1.25, visible output 5; hidden reasoning is not recorded).\n\n## Tally (1 game)\n| Asked for | Games | Impact | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A supported second-game path: a `Game` hook for step, frame, view and HUD instead of editing `sim.rs`, `frame.rs`, `build.rs` and `app.rs` | 1 | high | **done** (2026-10-16): `pav_core::games` (step, HUD text, status, bot), the starter game, `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`; drawing and HUD widgets still mean view or app code |\n| Entities, shots, health and AI that don't require rapier (`health.rs`, `projectile.rs`) | 1 | high | waiting |\n| A single-file web build (gzip + inline) | 1 | high | **done**: `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file` |\n| Primitive-part models drawn per frame from game state (props can't be) | 1 | medium | **done**: `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (plan row 4.1) |\n| Stock HUD widgets (bars, radar, off-screen markers, boss bar, banner) | 1 | medium | waiting |\n| Data-driven sound bank (`pav_audio::sounds_for` is a closed match) | 1 | medium | waiting |\n| Per-scene default camera on `load` | 1 | medium | waiting |\n| HUD visible to headless capture | 1 | medium | **done** (2026-10-19): captures draw the game UI (a hook game's HUD lines too); `ui` reads it as text |\n| Open top-down map data (roads, water, spawns) | 1 | low | waiting |\n| Continuous-play and top-down map captures | 1 | low | waiting |\n\n| Engine part | Used | Skipped |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Sim` (rewind, `state_hash`, snapshot), `pav_render::Scene`, particle presets, `pav_audio` synth, `pav_tools` capture/repl/input, the wasm shell and egui, `Rng`/`InputFrame`/bindings | 1 | 0 |\n| rapier entities, `character.rs`, `nav.rs`, `health.rs`, `projectile.rs`, `vehicle.rs` helicopter, `course.rs`, `zones.rs`, `stealth.rs`, `ai.rs` | 0 | 1 |\n| Rooms/TOML, puppet, moves, clips, the asset and creature studios (props, format 2) | 0 | 1 |\n\n## Games\n\n### Capital Strike, from Jungle Strike (1993; SNES, Genesis) — 2026-10-12\n- **Result:** one mission over Washington. Gun, rockets, homing missiles, flares and a dash;\n  armour, fuel and ammo; winch rescues; a motorcade escort; SAMs, AA guns, tanks, gunships and a\n  three-phase boss; loadouts, difficulties, checkpoints, radar and a procedural soundtrack.\n  `ai-hub/games/Jungle-Strike-Capital-Strike-2026-10-12-model-unknown.html` (9.8 MB); source as a\n  patch series against `6618522` in `ai-hub/sources/`.\n- **Agent:** Sonnet at medium effort, a subagent in its own worktree of this repo.\n- **Cost:** 1.65 h, 170 model calls, 54M tokens read (context grew from 56k to 485k), about 6.5M\n  weighted; 106k tokens of visible output. About 4,270 lines written, 3,965 of them game code\n  inside the engine crates.\n- **Verdict (agent's):** the renderer, wasm shell, audio synth and headless tools carried the\n  visuals and the first wasm build ran; the gameplay half didn't help a non-RPG, non-physics game,\n  so 4,000 lines went into a game layer with no engine facility, and the packaging was all its own.\n- **Checked:** the claims about `vehicle.rs` and `health.rs` hold (rapier-bound; no weapons,\n  damage or fuel), and AGENTS.md now says so. The game didn't touch props, format 2 or the studios:\n  its units are SDF and cube parts drawn in code, because a prop can't be drawn from game state.\n- **Acted on:** the single-file web build (every test game needs one), the AGENTS.md wording, and\n  the asset plan's row 4.1 now includes drawing a library asset from game code. Everything else\n  waits for a second game.\n","numLines":74,"startLine":1,"totalLines":74}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014V4sf5DfcrBWkLgY5z97JC","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj96NzQc31W4h1iMzXhh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S2wxVCcprG5BBGjcwF71yc","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/.scout/shardfall/docs/COOP_PLAN.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5883,"cache_read_input_tokens":59538,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5883},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33b9a197-b2fe-42e5-9a43-b32ae2dde858","uuid":"cb75eca0-c9b1-4a92-be04-b10cd386ed63","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:03.350Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj96Nk25ZgsUr2Lej92v","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01S2wxVCcprG5BBGjcwF71yc":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/.scout/shardfall/docs/COOP_PLAN.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S2wxVCcprG5BBGjcwF71yc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Co-op: up to four players, one world\n2\t\n3\tThe plan for online co-op in Shardfall: up to four players, each free to go anywhere, playing\n4\tthe same game as single player, with monsters made tougher instead of more numerous.\n5\t\n6\t**Status: on hold** (the user's call, 2026-10-17). The plan is kept for later, not scrapped;\n7\tnothing is built. Don't start any stage, or run §14's `/goal`, until the user takes it off hold.\n8\tWhen it resumes, re-check §3's facts against `main` first, since the code will have moved.\n9\t\n10\tFacts come from a survey of `main` at `dd5584b`; line numbers are as of then, and *unverified*\n11\tmarks what was not checked. The agent who builds it reads this whole file, then works through\n12\t§11's stages in order. Paths without a crate are under `crates/pav_core/src/`. Players get their\n13\town page, `docs/COOP.md`, in C5.\n14\t\n15\t## 1. What the user asked\n16\t| The ask | Where this plan meets it |\n17\t|---|---|\n18\t| **Up to four players**, online co-op, \"essentially the same single player experience\" | §2 (1-5), §5.1 (the cap), §5.5, §7 |\n19\t| **No extra monsters:** monster **life and damage scale** with the number of players | §6 |\n20\t| **Lag-free feel:** whatever it takes on every side (host, client, server) | §2 (2-4, 8-9), §5.7, §9, with measured targets in §10 |\n21\t| **No anti-cheat:** every client is trusted | §2 (4), §5.6: claims are never re-checked |\n22\t| **Free roaming:** anyone goes anywhere they can reach on their own, never tied to another player, never waiting (\"a super mini-MMO\") | §2 (1-2), §5.2: one shared instance per place, with travel gated only by the hero's own progress |\n23\t| **No voice or text chat.** Emotes with animations, pings and map pings are in | §8 |\n24\t| A short `/goal` to build it, the effort level, and whether to `/compact` or `/goal` first | §14 |\n25\t| Implied by `AGENTS.md`: the Windows `.exe`, Linux and the browser build play together | §9, §11 (C3) |\n26\t\n27\t## 2. The design in one page\n28\t1. **One game, one place, as today.** Each player's game simulates only the place its hero is\n29\t   in (town, a level, the arena, the lab). One `Sim` holds one place, and travel rebuilds it.\n30\t2. **Place owners.** The first player into a place builds it as single player does and **owns**\n31\t   it: their game runs its monsters, bosses and shared mechanics. Players who arrive later are\n32\t   **guests** whose games mirror the owner's. One guest is the place's **standby**. When the\n33\t   owner leaves, the standby takes over from an exact snapshot, confirmed before the owner goes;\n34\t   if the owner drops, the standby promotes from a snapshot at most 5 s old. A player alone in a\n35\t   place is playing single player, with zero lag.\n36\t3. **Your hero is yours.** Every game fully simulates its own hero: movement, skills, life,\n37\t   mana, potions, items, death. Input never waits on the network.\n38\t4. **The attacker decides it hit; the victim decides it was hit.** Your hits resolve on your\n39\t   screen against what you see and go to the owner as claims. A monster's attack resolves on\n40\t   its target's screen against where that hero really is. Nothing is re-checked (§5.6).\n41\t5. **Personal rewards, shared world.** Loot, gold, XP, boons, chest payouts, vendors, the stash\n42\t   and waypoints are per player and never cross the network: on a kill, each player nearby\n43\t   rolls their own drops, as in single player. Monsters, bosses, seals and the level's\n44\t   mechanics are shared (§5.5).\n45\t6. **Scaling:** life ×(1 + 0.75(n−1)) and damage ×(1 + 0.25(n−1)), where n is the number of\n46\t   players in the place (ruling E2; §6).\n47\t7. **Joining a place means receiving the owner's exact state.** The owner sends its `SimState`\n48\t   snapshot, the CBOR that `Sim::save_state` already writes, minus its own hero. Measured\n49\t   deflated with gzip: 41 KB for town, 81 KB for level 3, 109 KB for level 9 and 175 KB for\n50\t   level 14. Machines therefore never have to compute identical results.\n51\t8. **One replication pattern.** Every character simulated on another machine is a **movement\n52\t   stream** (interpolated) plus **action events** (casts, dodges, emotes, deaths), played\n53\t   through the engine's own animation and skills.\n54\t9. **Transport:** WebRTC data channels through matchbox, on native and in the browser: a full\n55\t   mesh with an unreliable channel for streams and reliable channels for control, events and\n56\t   bulk transfers (§5.4). Tests and tools use an in-process simulated network. The session host\n57\t   keeps only the directory of who owns which place, and hands it on under a new term if it\n58\t   leaves (§5.1).\n59\t10. **Online rules:** no pause, rewind or speed control. Hit-stop freezes only your hero and\n60\t    what you hit, never the world.\n61\t\n62\t**Rejected:**\n63\t- *One host simulating every place* (host-authoritative). Every guest would feel latency on\n64\t  every monster, even when alone, and the host's game would run up to five places at once.\n65\t- *Lockstep or rollback* (for example GGRS). Both need bit-identical simulation on Windows,\n66\t  Linux and wasm, which isn't true here: rapier's `enhanced-determinism` is off and the code\n67\t  uses `std` trig. Every player would also simulate every place.\n68\t- *A WebSocket relay.* On TCP, one lost packet stalls everything behind it, and every message\n69\t  takes two hops.\n70\t\n71\t## 3. What the code assumes today\n72\t| Area | Today | Where | Co-op change |\n73\t|---|---|---|---|\n74\t| The player | `SimState.player`; one `InputFrame` per tick, given only to the player | `sim.rs` `spawn_player` l.412, `step_inner` l.521 (input l.553) | Stays the local hero. Remote heroes use a new remote-drive mode (C0). |\n75\t| Shardfall's hero | `Game.hero` (the profile) and `hero_id`; live life and mana are in `Actor` | `arpg/mod.rs` l.123-126, `resume_game` l.324 | `Game` splits into a hero side and a place side (C0). |\n76\t| Monster AI | `Game::pre` takes one hero: wake at 12 m, pack at 9 m, casts at the hero's feet; one flow field cached per hero cell | `arpg/mod.rs` `pre` l.629-729, `arpg/brain.rs` l.11-12 and `think` l.56, `arpg/mechanics.rs` `hero_flow` l.248 | Target the nearest living hero (sticky); one flow field per targeted hero. |\n77\t| Engine checks | one `player_feet` for every engine brain; `PLAYER_CRUMBLE` tiles react only to the player | `sim.rs` l.545, `ai.rs`, `destruct.rs` l.78 | Any hero (the Crumbling Halls need it). |\n78\t| Time | hit-stop slows the whole sim to 0.06 | `Game::time_scale`, `arpg/mod.rs` l.271; `sim.rs` l.524 | Per entity online (§7). |\n79\t| Pause, rewind, speed | the app's `TimeControl`; the Esc menu pauses; Backspace rewinds | `pav_app/src/simhost.rs` l.20-38; `app.rs` l.764, l.1000 | Off online. |\n80\t| Travel | rebuilds the whole `SimState` within one tick, carrying the `Game`; `resume_game` wipes actors, loot and the level; the layout seed comes from the sim's RNG, so a level is new on every visit | `arpg/mod.rs` `game_travel` l.404; `arpg/world.rs` l.513 | Kept. Co-op decides whether to build fresh (owner) or adopt the owner's snapshot (guest). |\n81\t| Damage | `Game::hit` for everything; `targets()` filters by `Team::hostile`, so there is no friendly fire | `arpg/mod.rs` l.1077; `arpg/skills.rs` `fire_cast` l.455, `targets` l.416 | One routing check at the top of `Game::hit` (§5.6). |\n82\t| Rewards | `kill()` gives XP to the one `g.hero`; `drop_loot` uses that hero's find stats | `arpg/mod.rs` l.1309; `arpg/loot.rs` l.50 | Each game runs its own reward on a `Killed` event. |\n83\t| Scaling hooks | `spawn_actor` sets life (l.598) and damage (l.611); `enemy_damage` is also applied in `roll_damage`, the boss's `death_burst` and powers | `arpg/mod.rs` l.578; `arpg/skills.rs` l.331; `arpg/boss.rs` l.350; `arpg/powers.rs` l.251, l.348 | Life scales on the owner, damage on the victim (§6). |\n84\t| Where you can go | `GameCmd::Travel` is gated by the hero's own `max_depth`; `use_exit` refuses while the level is sealed | `arpg/cmd.rs` l.296; `arpg/mechanics.rs` l.911 | Unchanged: \"anywhere they can reach on their own\". |\n85\t| HUD and map | the map takes the hero to be the first `Team::Hero` actor | `pav_app/src/arpg_ui.rs` `level_hud` l.562 (l.641) | Use `hero_id`; draw remote heroes and pings. |\n86\t| View | `RenderFrame.player` and `focus` are single; every entity carries its own puppet look | `sim.rs` `frame` l.941; `pav_view/src/build.rs` l.1104 | Unchanged (the local hero); four heroes already render. |\n87\t| Saves | one hero and roster per machine or browser | `pav_app/src/save.rs` | Unchanged: no save crosses the network. |\n88\t| Bots and tools | `autoplay` and `campaign` drive `g.hero` in one `Session` | `arpg/bot.rs`; `pav_tools/src/agent_tools.rs` l.227 | Per player, through the `coop` tool (§10). |\n89\t| App | no title screen (boots into `town`); the pause menu is the only menu | `pav_app/src/settings.rs` l.172; `ui.rs` `MenuAction` l.25, `pause_menu` l.167 | The co-op entries go in the pause menu. |\n90\t| Networking | none; no async runtime in use (tokio is only in `Cargo.lock`) | every `Cargo.toml` | A new crate, `pav_net` (§4). |\n91\t| Several sims | already work in one process (travel builds a second `Sim`); content caches are process-global | `arpg/mod.rs` l.409 | Enough for the in-process test network. |\n92\t\n93\t## 4. Where the code goes\n94\t- **`pav_core::net`** (new; engine API):\n95\t  - `PeerId`, `NetId` and `Channel` (unreliable or reliable).\n96\t  - A tick's `NetInbox` and `NetOutbox`.\n97\t  - `InterpBuffer`: interpolates, extrapolates up to a limit, then holds.\n98\t  - A link clock: RTT, jitter and the remote-tick estimate.\n99\t  - `SimNet`: an in-process network whose links have latency, jitter, loss and bandwidth drawn\n100\t    from a seed.\n101\t\n102\t  It is pure: no IO, no threads, safe on wasm. It is written for any game on the hook; Shardfall\n103\t  uses it first.\n104\t- **`pav_core::arpg::coop`** (new; the game): messages, the directory, owner and guest logic,\n105\t  the hero and place streams, damage routing, scaling, pings and emotes. Its state is\n106\t  `Game.net: Option<Box<CoopState>>`, inside `SimState`, so a player's own snapshots and\n107\t  replays carry it. It has two parts:\n108\t  - the **session part** (directory, peers, host term, link clocks) lasts the whole session;\n109\t  - the **place part** (role, place epoch, `NetId` map, interpolation buffers, pending events\n110\t    and claims, watermarks) belongs to one place.\n111\t\n112\t  Place snapshots sent to other players carry neither part. Adopting a snapshot keeps the\n113\t  recipient's session part and builds a fresh place part from the snapshot; so does any travel\n114\t  (§5.2).\n115\t- **`pav_net`** (new crate; internals, doctrine 8): the WebRTC transport (matchbox, on native and\n116\t  wasm) and the signaling server with rooms (native only; §9), both embedded in the host's game\n117\t  and as a small `shardfall-signal` binary. It handles bytes only and knows nothing of the game,\n118\t  so `pav_core` stays free of IO.\n119\t- **`pav_app`**: flags and settings, the net pump, the Co-op window, party frames, ping and emote\n120\t  input and wheels, and pad and touch bindings.\n121\t- **`pav_view`**: name plates and ping markers, drawn in the 3D scene so captures show them.\n122\t- **`pav_tools`**: one `coop` tool. The MCP tool list is at its 48,000-character cap\n123\t  (`pav_tools/tests/asset_errors.rs` l.101).\n124\t- **Encoding:**\n125\t  - Messages use `postcard` 1.1.3: compact serde, a new dependency.\n126\t  - Snapshots use the existing CBOR (`Sim::save_state`, `ciborium`), deflated with\n127\t    `miniz_oxide` 0.8, already a workspace dependency.\n128\t  - A malformed message is dropped and logged, never a panic.\n129\t\n130\t## 5. How it works\n131\t### 5.1 Session and directory\n132\t- A session is a matchbox full-mesh room at `<signal>/<code>`. The code is six characters of\n133\t  Crockford base32. Every player links to every other: at most six links. Rooms are kept apart\n134\t  by our signaling server (§9), and `Hello` carries the code as a second check.\n135\t- **Handshake:** a joining player sends `Hello{protocol, build, code, name, profile}`. The host\n136\t  answers `Welcome{slot, directory, settings}` or `Reject{reason}`:\n137\t  - The reason is a version mismatch (the `.exe` and the browser build can differ; bump\n138\t    `PROTOCOL` whenever a message changes), a wrong code, or a full session (a fifth player).\n139\t  - Failures show on screen in plain words: host not found, version mismatch, session full.\n140\t- **The host** is the room's creator. It keeps the **directory**:\n141\t  - peers: slot 1-4, colour, name, and a hero profile (character, level, look);\n142\t  - places: `PlaceKey` → owner, standby, members, epoch (the standby is the member who takes\n143\t    over if the owner drops; §5.2);\n144\t  - the session settings: the host's difficulty sliders and the `coop.*` params.\n145\t\n146\t  It broadcasts every change, and every player keeps a copy.\n147\t- **If the host leaves or drops,** copies of the directory may differ (a broadcast can stop\n148\t  partway), so the new host rebuilds it rather than trusting its copy:\n149\t  - The earliest-joined connected player (ties by peer id) becomes host under a new **host\n150\t    term**: the pair (number, host's peer id).\n151\t    - Every control message carries the term, and messages from older terms are dropped.\n152\t    - If a split link ever leaves two hosts, the higher pair wins; the other host steps down\n153\t      and reports.\n154\t  - The new host asks every player for `Report{place, role, place epoch, name, profile,\n155\t    settings version}` and rebuilds the directory from the answers:\n156\t    - if two players claim to own one place, the higher place epoch wins and the other resyncs\n157\t      as a guest;\n158\t    - the settings with the highest version win.\n159\t  - It publishes the rebuilt directory before accepting any change.\n160\t\n161\t  With the embedded signaling server (E1's default), nobody new can join after the host goes,\n162\t  but the session goes on.\n163\t- **`PlaceKey`** is one of `Town`, `Arena`, `Lab` or `Level(depth)`. There is one instance per\n164\t  key per session: whoever goes to depth 7 joins whoever is already there.\n165\t\n166\t### 5.2 Entering, leaving and owning a place\n167\t**Entering.** Travel starts as today: the portal window, `T`, or the exit, through\n168\t`GameCmd::Travel`, gated by the hero's own `max_depth`. Then:\n169\t1. The game sends `Enter(place)` to the host and keeps its loading veil up.\n170\t2. **Empty place:** the host answers `Assign{owner: you, epoch}`. The game builds the place as\n171\t   single player does, and owns it.\n172\t3. **Occupied place:** the host answers `Assign{owner: O}`. The game asks O to `Join`.\n173\t   - O sends `PlaceSnapshot`: its `SimState` minus its hero side and its `Game.net`, deflated,\n174\t     in 16 KB chunks on the bulk channel (about 0.1-0.5 s).\n175\t   - The game **adopts** it: it keeps its own hero side and co-op state, turns O and the other\n176\t     members into remote heroes, and spawns its hero at the place's spawn. It is now a guest.\n177\t4. O adds the newcomer's hero, and every member gets `MemberJoined`.\n178\t\n179\t**Leaving** is travelling away or disconnecting:\n180\t- **A guest leaves:** the owner removes its hero, and members get `MemberLeft`.\n181\t- **Every place with guests has a standby:** its earliest-joined guest, named in the directory;\n182\t  if the standby leaves, the next guest takes the role. The owner keeps the standby promotable:\n183\t  - it sends the standby the place stream without the interest limit (far actors at 2 Hz);\n184\t  - it sends a full snapshot every 5 s, or every 10 s once snapshots pass 100 KB;\n185\t  - this owner-to-standby link has its own budget of 40 KB/s, on top of §5.4's per-player\n186\t    budget.\n187\t- **The owner leaves, others remain:** the standby takes over in three steps, so no change is\n188\t  lost:\n189\t  1. The old owner takes a final snapshot and sends it as `Handoff`. From that tick it stops\n190\t     advancing the place: monsters hold, and claims and uses queue unapplied. So nothing\n191\t     happens after the snapshot that the snapshot lacks.\n192\t  2. The standby adopts it and answers `Ready`.\n193\t  3. The old owner tells the host, which broadcasts `OwnerChanged{epoch+1}` to re-point\n194\t     everyone's streams. The old owner forwards its queued claims and uses to the new owner,\n195\t     then travels behind its loading veil, and forwards any claim that still reaches it.\n196\t\n197\t  Monsters pause for a fraction of a second at a handoff.\n198\t\n199\t  If `Ready` hasn't come within 3 s, the old owner travels anyway and the host treats it as a\n200\t  drop.\n201\t- **The owner drops:** after `owner_timeout` (2 s) with no packets, the host makes the standby\n202\t  the owner. The standby **promotes**: it adopts its latest standby snapshot, applies its fresher\n203\t  stream data on top, and switches the mirrors to full AI. Brains restart, and up to 5 s of\n204\t  far-off changes can be lost: acceptable for a crash.\n205\t- **The last player leaves:** the place is gone. The next visitor builds a new one, as in single\n206\t  player.\n207\t\n208\t**Epochs** drop stale messages from a former owner. If two players enter an empty place\n209\ttogether, the host answers in order and the second waits for the first's snapshot.\n210\t\n211\t**Resync** heals any desync. A guest asks for a fresh snapshot and adopts it, keeping its hero,\n212\twhen any of these happens:\n213\t- a `NetId` named by a stream or an event still has no `Spawned` 2 s after it first appeared.\n214\t  A stream, or a `Cast` on the unordered event channel, can beat its spawn; until then it is\n215\t  buffered, not treated as a desync;\n216\t- a `PlaceState` hash doesn't match;\n217\t- an epoch has a gap.\n218\t\n219\tOne adopt function serves joining, handoff, promotion and resync.\n220\t\n221\t**IDs:** the owner assigns each actor's `NetId`, and each game maps `NetId`s to its own\n222\t`EntityId`s, because games allocate entities independently after they adopt a snapshot. Remote\n223\theroes are `NetId`s by slot.\n224\t\n225\t### 5.3 One tick\n226\t1. The app receives packets and stamps each with the next local tick, giving the `NetInbox`.\n227\t2. `Sim::step_net(&InputFrame, &NetInbox)` runs three steps:\n228\t   - the co-op pre-step applies the inbox: directory, snapshots, events and claims, and\n229\t     streams into the interpolation buffers;\n230\t   - the normal step;\n231\t   - the co-op post-step writes the `NetOutbox`: streams and events.\n232\t3. The app sends the outbox.\n233\t\n234\tReplays record the inbox per tick in a new `net` track in `Replay`. So `record_save` and\n235\t`replay` still reproduce one player's game exactly on the machine that recorded it (doctrine 5;\n236\t`AGENTS.md`'s platform note). Single player calls `step_net` with an empty inbox and behaves\n237\texactly as today.\n238\t\n239\t### 5.4 Streams and events\n240\tFour channels, so a lost or large message never holds up an unrelated one:\n241\t- **0 streams:** unreliable and unordered, each message at most 1,200 bytes.\n242\t- **1 control:** reliable and ordered: the handshake, the directory, owner changes and the\n243\t  handoff's steps.\n244\t- **2 events:** reliable and unordered. Each event carries a per-sender sequence number, and\n245\t  order is restored only where it matters:\n246\t  - a `NetId`'s spawn, death and despawn are kept as tombstones, so a late spawn never revives\n247\t    a dead actor;\n248\t  - each player's claims apply in id order (§5.6).\n249\t- **3 bulk:** reliable and ordered: snapshots and `PlaceState`.\n250\t\n251\t| Message | Ch. | When | From → to | Carries |\n252\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n253\t| Hero stream | 0 | 30 Hz | each player → others in its place | tick, position, velocity, facing, grounded and posture, life and mana fractions, ailment bits, current action and phase |\n254\t| Place stream | 0 | 20 Hz | owner → each guest | owner tick and, per player, the last claim applied (the claim watermark); then per actor in the guest's interest: `NetId`, position, velocity, yaw, life fraction, state bits (casting, stunned, frozen, burning, dead), target |\n255\t| Standby stream, standby snapshot | 0, 3 | far actors 2 Hz; snapshot every 5-10 s | owner → the standby | the place stream without the interest limit; a full snapshot (§5.2; on its own 40 KB/s budget) |\n256\t| `Cast`, `Dodge` | 2 | on event | whoever runs the character → its place | actor, skill, aim, target, start tick, seed |\n257\t| `Spawned`, `Despawned` | 2 | on event | owner → guests | `NetId`, and the new entity and its `Actor` as in the owner's state (CBOR), so summons, waves and brood arrive complete. Every reference inside them to another entity (`Actor.master`, `last_hit`, hit lists, owners, joints) travels as a `NetId` and is remapped on receipt; an unknown one becomes `None` |\n258\t| `Killed` | 2 | on event | owner → guests | `NetId`, owner tick, killer, position, level, rarity, rank, boss |\n259\t| `HitClaim` | 2 | per hit | guest → owner | claim id (rising, per claimant), target, and the attacker's side as its game resolved it: damage by type before the target's mitigation, crit, ailments with strength and duration, knockback, execute, power effects |\n260\t| `UseClaim` | 2 | per use | guest → owner | a world-changing use: a chest, a well, a keg, a Menagerie creature, an arena wave |\n261\t| `HeroHit`, `HeroDied`, `Respawned` | 2 | on event | a player → its place | others' screens: flash, number, fall |\n262\t| `PlaceState` | 3 | on change, ≤ 5 Hz | owner → guests | the shared mechanics state, deflated and sequence-numbered (latest wins) |\n263\t| `PartyStatus` | 2 | 1 Hz | a player → all | life, level, place, dead (for party frames) |\n264\t| `ProfileChanged` | 2 | on change | a player → all | character, level, look |\n265\t| `Emote`, `Ping` | 2 | on use | a player → its place | §8 |\n266\t| `Enter`, `Assign`, `Join`, `Handoff`, `Ready`, `OwnerChanged`, `Report` | 1 (`Handoff` on 3) | on change | the players concerned | the host term and place epoch, plus each step's data (§5.1, §5.2) |\n267\t| `Probe`, `Echo` | 0 | 1 Hz per link | both ways | the link clock |\n268\t\n269\tThe place stream covers actors in the guest's interest: within 45 m of its hero, every boss,\n270\tand anything targeting it. It sends the nearest first and goes round-robin past the size cap.\n271\t\n272\tBudget: at most 40 KB/s per player with four players in one level, not counting the standby's\n273\tlink, which has its own budget (§5.2). Positions start as f32; quantize them only if a\n274\tmeasurement goes over budget.\n275\t\n276\t### 5.5 Who simulates what\n277\t| Thing | Simulated by | Other games |\n278\t|---|---|---|\n279\t| Each hero | its player's game, fully | remote-drive from the hero stream; casts, dodges and emotes from events; its damage ignored |\n280\t| Monsters, bosses, summons, chest keepers, arena waves, Menagerie creatures | the place's owner (AI, life, ailments, deaths) | mirrors: remote-drive from the place stream; casts from events |\n281\t| Projectiles and ground effects | every game that plays the cast | damage only as §5.6 routes it |\n282\t| Shared mechanics: kegs, rift gates, wells, totems, the crumbling floor, the seal and exit, chests and their waves | the owner | `PlaceState` and events (a keg's blast: each game applies it to its own hero) |\n283\t| Static hazards: lava, spike beats, wind, ice, darkness, time bubbles | every game, on its own hero (the owner also on monsters) | not sent |\n284\t| Personal: loot, gold, XP, potions, shrine boons, chest payouts, the passive point for a first boss clear, vendor and buyback, the stash, waypoints (`max_depth`), fog of war (`LevelState.seen`), the kill streak | each player's game only | never sent |\n285\t| Cosmetic: villagers, Biscuit, the Hall of Heroes performers, floaters, gibs, particles, shake | each game locally | never sent |\n286\t\n287\tShrines are personal: each player can use each shrine once. A guest's world-changing use plays\n288\tat once in its own game and goes to the owner as a `UseClaim`.\n289\t\n290\t*Unverified:* how time bubbles and wind act on characters. In C2, check each mechanic in\n291\t`arpg/mechanics.rs` against this table, and record any changes in the stage's `docs/HISTORY.md`\n292\tentry.\n293\t\n294\t### 5.6 Damage routing\n295\tOne check at the top of `Game::hit` returns `Apply`, `Claim` or `Ignore`:\n296\t\n297\t| Target ↓ \\ attacker → | your hero | a monster or hazard | a remote hero |\n298\t|---|---|---|---|\n299\t| your hero | — (no friendly fire) | **Apply**: you decide you were hit | — |\n300\t| a monster or object, in a place you own | **Apply** | **Apply** | **Ignore**: its player claims |\n301\t| a monster or object, in a place where you're a guest | **Claim**: play it now, send `HitClaim` | **Ignore** | **Ignore** |\n302\t| a remote hero | — | **Ignore**: its player decides | — |\n303\t\n304\tThe owner applies a claim through `Game::hit`:\n305\t- **The attacker's side is used as sent:** no re-roll, and no reading of the attacker's powers\n306\t  or build, which the owner doesn't have.\n307\t- **The owner applies its own current defences for the target:** armour, resistances, wards\n308\t  and buffs. The guest predicted with its mirror's defences; the watermark corrects any\n309\t  difference.\n310\t- **Order:** it applies each player's claims in id order, holding any that arrive early. The\n311\t  watermark it advertises is the last claim applied, so it never passes a gap.\n312\t- It credits the claimer.\n313\t\n314\tEffects on the attacker (life and mana on hit, power procs, hit-stop) happen in the attacker's\n315\tgame when the hit lands there. Single player is the \"a place you own\" row.\n316\t\n317\t### 5.7 Lag hiding\n318\t1. **Your own hero has no latency** (§2, point 3).\n319\t2. **Other players' characters show at the owner's time minus an interpolation delay:** 1.5\n320\t   times the stream interval plus the p95 jitter, clamped to 50-200 ms. On loss they\n321\t   extrapolate for up to 150 ms, then hold. Corrections blend over 100 ms; teleports (rift\n322\t   gates, respawn) snap.\n323\t3. **Hits are instant on your screen.** The flash, number and sound, your hit-stop and your\n324\t   life on hit all happen at once. The monster's bar shows predicted life: the owner's latest\n325\t   figure minus your claims above the watermark that came with it, so no claim is subtracted\n326\t   twice. A predicted kill plays the death and drops your loot at once, since loot is personal.\n327\t   If the watermark hasn't passed the killing claim within 1 s, the mirror comes back (rare).\n328\t4. **You dodge what you see.** Monster attacks resolve on your machine against your hero, with\n329\t   the full windup. It is never shortened, which is fair to the victim.\n330\t5. **Interactions** (rift gate, well, keg, chest) play at once in your game; the owner confirms.\n331\t6. **The link clock** sends a probe on each link every second and keeps RTT, jitter (EWMA) and\n332\t   each place's owner-tick estimate.\n333\t7. **Heroes pass through each other**; monsters still collide with heroes.\n334\t8. **The owner is whoever entered first.** If phones or slow browsers owning a place make\n335\t   guests' monsters stutter, hand ownership to a native player in the place. Build this only if\n336\t   it shows up.\n337\t\n338\t## 6. Scaling\n339\t- **n** is the number of players in the place (E2's default). Setting `coop.scale_by = \"session\"`\n340\t  counts everyone in the session instead.\n341\t- **Life** is multiplied by 1 + `coop.life_per_player` × (n−1). The default 0.75 gives ×1,\n342\t  ×1.75, ×2.5 and ×3.25.\n343\t  - The owner applies it in `spawn_actor` (`arpg/mod.rs` l.598).\n344\t  - When n changes, every game rescales the actors it holds in the same way, keeping each\n345\t    life fraction (`Actor::recompute`, `arpg/combat.rs` l.295): the owner its monsters, guests\n346\t    their mirrors. So predictions, execute thresholds and damage numbers use the owner's\n347\t    maximum.\n348\t- **Damage** is multiplied by 1 + `coop.damage_per_player` × (n−1). The default 0.25 gives ×1,\n349\t  ×1.25, ×1.5 and ×1.75.\n350\t  - It is applied where monster damage is rolled in the victim's game, using that game's n,\n351\t    next to `enemy_damage`.\n352\t  - The places are `roll_damage` (`arpg/skills.rs` l.331), `death_burst` (`arpg/boss.rs`\n353\t    l.350) and powers (`arpg/powers.rs` l.251, l.348).\n354\t- **Rewards** are per player, as single player gives them. On `Killed`, every player within\n355\t  `coop.share_radius` (40 m) of the kill, or who hit the monster, gets the full XP and rolls\n356\t  their own drops. Distance is judged at the kill's owner tick: each game keeps its hero's\n357\t  positions for the last 2 s, and judges an older kill from its oldest kept position.\n358\t- Ward Totems and kegs keep their own life, as today.\n359\t- The session host's difficulty sliders apply in every game.\n360\t- All four params are a `Tunable` (`coop.*`), so `params`, `set`, presets and the tuning panel\n361\t  get them.\n362\t- **Check (C2):** compare four bots in a place against one bot alone. If kill time or deaths\n363\t  differ by more than 25%, adjust the defaults once and record it. Feel is the user's call.\n364\t\n365\t## 7. Online changes to single-player features\n366\t- **Pause, speed, single step and rewind** (the Esc menu, F6, Backspace, the pad's Select): off in\n367\t  a session. The Esc menu opens over the running game.\n368\t- **Hit-stop:** `Game::time_scale` returns 1 in a session. Your hero freezes for the hit-stop's\n369\t  length, and the struck monster's pose holds on your screen. The boss entrance's 0.35 s freeze\n370\t  becomes a camera beat.\n371\t- **Studios, the F10 editor and the engine demo scenes** (world, rooms, starter): leave the\n372\t  session first. Co-op covers Shardfall's places only.\n373\t- **Character switching** in the Hall of Heroes: allowed in town; it sends `ProfileChanged`.\n374\t- **Death:** as single player: 3 s, then respawn at the place's spawn with full life. Other\n375\t  players see you fall and reappear.\n376\t- **Saves:** each game saves its own hero, as now. Rewind history isn't recorded online.\n377\t\n378\t## 8. Pings, emotes and the co-op UI\n379\t**Pings.** The keys below are free in the game today (`pav_app/src/input.rs` l.111-129,\n380\t`app.rs` l.835-944).\n381\t- **World pings:** `V` (or mouse Back) pings the ground under the cursor, and the context picks\n382\t  the kind:\n383\t  - a monster → **Danger**;\n384\t  - an item, feature or spot → **Look**;\n385\t  - your own hero → **Help**;\n386\t  - anything else → **Go**.\n387\t\n388\t  Hold `V` for a wheel of the four. On a pad, R3 pings at the aim point (hold it for the wheel).\n389\t  On touch, a ping button sits in the top-right row (`touch.rs` l.410-442).\n390\t- **Map pings:** with the big map open (`M`), click while holding `V` (or `Alt`) to ping that\n391\t  spot.\n392\t- **What the others see** (players in the same place only):\n393\t  - a ring and beam in the pinger's colour, with the kind's icon, for 4 s, drawn by `pav_view`\n394\t    so captures show them;\n395\t  - an arrow at the screen edge with the distance, when the ping is off screen;\n396\t  - a blip on the minimap and the big map;\n397\t  - a short synth cue per kind (`pav_audio`).\n398\t- **Limit:** at most 3 pings per 3 s per player; extras are dropped.\n399\t\n400\t**Emotes.**\n401\t- **Eight**, defined as data in `game/emotes.toml` (key, name, clip, loop, icon): wave, cheer,\n402\t  bow, dance, laugh, point, sit and taunt.\n403\t  - Clips come from the store (`assets shelf=clip`, `assets find=<word> kind=clip`), keeping\n404\t    each source's credit and licence: open-licence tiers only, never `anim/local`.\n405\t  - A missing clip is made through the Animation Studio's loop.\n406\t- **Use:** hold `B` for the emote wheel (pad: L3; touch: an emote button). The emote plays on\n407\t  your hero at once and stops when you move, attack or dodge. Other players' games play it on\n408\t  your hero too.\n409\t- *Unverified:* that every clip plays on all three heroes' puppets. Check with `animsheet` in\n410\t  C4.\n411\t\n412\t**UI** (egui in `pav_app`; the `coop` tool reports the same state as text, because captures\n413\tdon't show egui):\n414\t- **Pause menu → Co-op:**\n415\t  - **Host:** the code, a copy button, and \"players n/4\".\n416\t  - **Join:** a code, or `CODE@ip:port` for a host's embedded server.\n417\t  - **Leave.**\n418\t- **Flags and settings:**\n419\t  - flags: `--host`, `--join CODE[@addr]` and `--name NAME`;\n420\t  - `shardfall.toml`: `name`, `signal = \"wss://...\"`, `signal_port = 3536`, `turn`,\n421\t    `turn_user` and `turn_pass`;\n422\t  - the browser: `?join=CODE`.\n423\t\n424\t  The game still boots into town; hosting turns the running game into a session.\n425\t- **Party frames**, up to three: slot colour, name, character, level, a life bar, the place\n426\t  (\"Emberwatch\", \"Depth 7, The Molten Floor\"), a dead mark, and a link-quality dot.\n427\t- **Name plates** over remote heroes (`pav_view`): name and level in the slot colour, with a thin\n428\t  life bar.\n429\t- **Map:** remote heroes as arrows in their colours, and pings.\n430\t- **Toasts** for joins, leaves and lost connections. The text is fixed; there is no chat.\n431\t- **Slot colours:** 1 amber `#f2a33a`, 2 cyan `#3ac8f2`, 3 lime `#8fe04a`, 4 magenta `#e04ad0`.\n432\t\n433\t## 9. Transport and connection\n434\t- **The libraries:** **matchbox_socket 0.12.0** (2025-05-21) and **matchbox_signaling 0.12.0**.\n435\t  - They are the newest versions at least 12 months old on 2026-10-16 (doctrine 9; 0.14 is\n436\t    current). Licence: MIT or Apache-2.0.\n437\t  - The same API runs on native and wasm: full-mesh WebRTC data channels with\n438\t    `ChannelConfig::unreliable()`, `reliable()` and custom configs (reliable and unordered is\n439\t    `ordered: false` with unlimited retransmits), ICE servers (`RtcIceServerConfig`, Google\n440\t    STUN by default), and a pluggable `SignallerBuilder`.\n441\t  - Native-to-browser play is a stated matchbox feature, *unverified* here until C3.\n442\t- **Rooms:** the signaling library's built-in full mesh (`full_mesh_builder`) puts every\n443\t  connected peer in one mesh. The standalone `matchbox_server` keeps rooms apart by URL path,\n444\t  using its own topology on the signaling library; its newest qualifying release is 0.11.0, and\n445\t  it has no 0.12. `pav_net` reuses that pattern on `matchbox_signaling` 0.12, so the host's\n446\t  embedded server and the public `shardfall-signal` binary run the same code.\n447\t- **Native:**\n448\t  - It uses webrtc-rs 0.13, with `ring` 0.17 and `rustls` and no OpenSSL; matchbox's lockfile\n449\t    lists about 300 crates.\n450\t  - One `net` thread runs a tokio runtime. On it run matchbox's message loop (which reaches\n451\t    tokio through `async-compat`) and, on the host, the embedded signaling server (axum on\n452\t    tokio).\n453\t  - The sim thread drains and fills the socket's channels each tick.\n454\t  - Check the Windows cross-build (`scripts/build-windows.sh`) on the first day of C3.\n455\t- **wasm** uses the browser's own WebRTC. The message loop runs under\n456\t  `wasm_bindgen_futures::spawn_local`, and the socket is polled in `pump()`\n457\t  (`pav_app/src/app.rs` l.990). No threads and no blocking calls.\n458\t- **Signaling (E1):**\n459\t  - By default, the host's native game runs `pav_net`'s signaling server on port 3536.\n460\t    Joiners use `CODE@host:3536` on a LAN, through a port forward, or over a VPN such as\n461\t    Tailscale.\n462\t  - A browser page served over https can only reach a `wss://` server.\n463\t    Internet play from the browser waits on E1.\n464\t  - Recommended: `shardfall-signal` in a small container with TLS, its URL in\n465\t    `shardfall.toml` and the web build.\n466\t- **NAT:** STUN only by default. TURN comes from settings if a real session fails to connect;\n467\t  most home networks connect without it.\n468\t- **Sizes:** unreliable messages are at most 1,200 bytes; snapshots go in 16 KB chunks on the\n469\t  bulk channel.\n470\t\n471\t## 10. Tools, tests and targets\n472\t- **`SimNet`:** N players in one process, each with a full `Sim`, over links with seeded latency,\n473\t  jitter, loss and bandwidth. One tick steps every game and the network. Runs are deterministic,\n474\t  so tests and tools repeat exactly.\n475\t- **The `coop` tool** (one tool, help at most 50 tokens):\n476\t  - `coop players=4 latency=80 jitter=15 loss=0.02` starts an in-process session; player 1 is\n477\t    the current session.\n478\t  - `coop peer=2` points the other tools (`input`, `player`, `capture`, `see`, `game_cmd`,\n479\t    `level`) at player 2's game; `step` advances every game.\n480\t  - `coop` with no arguments reports, for each player: place, role, life, RTT, KB/s,\n481\t    interpolation delay, mirror error, unconfirmed claims and resyncs.\n482\t  - `coop bots=true seconds=60` lets bots drive every player, and reports kills, deaths, XP and\n483\t    clear times with the network numbers.\n484\t  - Through `pav live` it reports the real session.\n485\t- **Targets.** These are objective stand-ins for \"feels lag-free\", measured at 100 ms RTT, ±20 ms\n486\t  jitter and 2% loss, with four players in one level:\n487\t\n488\t  | Measure | Target |\n489\t  |---|---|\n490\t  | Own-hero input latency | 0 ticks (true by construction; asserted) |\n491\t  | Mirror error: a guest's mirror vs the owner's actor at the same owner tick | median ≤ 0.15 m, p99 ≤ 0.5 m |\n492\t  | Pops: corrections over 0.5 m in one frame | ≤ 1 per minute per player |\n493\t  | Bandwidth | ≤ 40 KB/s per player |\n494\t  | Hit confirmation (the watermark passing a claim) | median ≤ RTT + 2 ticks, p99 ≤ 2 × RTT |\n495\t  | A guest's tick cost | ≤ the owner's |\n496\t- **Suites** in `crates/pav_core/tests/coop.rs` (new; cargo, no GPU):\n497\t  - the handshake: version mismatch, a full session;\n498\t  - entering, joining, leaving, handoff, owner drop and host drop;\n499\t  - a graceful handoff loses no change; an owner drop with players far apart promotes the\n500\t    standby without stale actors; a host drop in mid-broadcast converges on one directory;\n501\t  - a place stream that beats its `Spawned` is buffered, not resynced;\n502\t  - the claim watermark: predicted life never subtracts a claim twice, and claims that arrive\n503\t    out of order never move the watermark past a gap;\n504\t  - a handoff in mid-fight loses no claim;\n505\t  - a spawned brood's `master` points at the right actor in every game;\n506\t  - travelling between two occupied places leaves no stale `NetId`;\n507\t  - a guest's kill confirmed by the owner, with the guest's own loot;\n508\t  - victim-side hits: a dodge on the guest's screen counts;\n509\t  - scaling, and the live rescale;\n510\t  - ping and emote delivery;\n511\t  - one player's replay reproducing its hash;\n512\t  - every existing suite, unchanged.\n513\t- **`pav_net` tests:** two native peers and the embedded signaling server on 127.0.0.1 exchange\n514\t  messages on all four channels, and two rooms on one server stay apart.\n515\t- **`scripts/coop-local.sh`** (new), all on localhost, with screenshots from each window into\n516\t  `out/coop/`:\n517\t  - a native host under Xvfb and lavapipe, with `--bridge 127.0.0.1:7878`;\n518\t  - a native joiner on `:7879`;\n519\t  - a browser tab in headless Chromium with `?join=` (the flags are in `docs/REFERENCE.md`,\n520\t    \"Browser and phones\").\n521\t- **Windows:** the `.exe` hosting under Wine (`scripts/smoke-windows.sh`) with a Linux joiner.\n522\t- **Cost:** these are tool checks with no model calls, so they may run as often as needed\n523\t  (`AGENTS.md`, \"Testing budget\"). No agent judges; looks and feel go to the user.\n524\t\n525\t## 11. Stages\n526\tEach stage is one PR, merged when its checks pass (`AGENTS.md`, \"Per PR\"), with a dated\n527\t`docs/HISTORY.md` entry and `docs/PROGRESS.md` brought up to date. Single player must behave\n528\texactly as before after every stage.\n529\t\n530\t**C0: several heroes in one place (no network).**\n531\t- **Remote-drive mode** for characters (`Character.remote`): a kinematic target, with the\n532\t  animation fed from velocity, posture and grounded. The `riding` early-out (`character.rs`\n533\t  l.494) is the template, but it skips animation, which this mode keeps.\n534\t- **Remote heroes** as `Team::Hero` actors with their own looks. Generalize `refresh_hero`\n535\t  (`arpg/mod.rs` l.441) to any hero profile.\n536\t- **AI for several heroes:** monsters target the nearest living hero, sticky with hysteresis;\n537\t  `hero_flow` is kept per targeted hero; aggro checks every hero. `PLAYER_CRUMBLE` and the\n538\t  other player-only engine checks take any hero.\n539\t- **Split `Game`:**\n540\t  - The hero side is `hero`, `hero_id` and the hero's `Actor`, `loot`, `gold`, `vendor`,\n541\t    `buyback`, `respawn`, `travel`, `floaters`, `message`, `hitstop`, `shake`, `streak`,\n542\t    `echoes`, `power_clock`, `level_flash`, the loot settings, the bags cache and `roster`.\n543\t  - The rest is the place side.\n544\t  - Add `adopt_place(snapshot, hero_side)`, and have travel use it too.\n545\t- **The damage-routing check** (§5.6) with the single-player role, and the `coop.*` params with\n546\t  the scaling functions.\n547\t- **Exit:**\n548\t  - every suite passes unchanged;\n549\t  - `campaign from=1 to=3` matches `main`;\n550\t  - a new test adds a second, remote-driven hero, and monsters go for the nearer one.\n551\t\n552\t**C1: a session over the simulated network.**\n553\t- `pav_core::net`: `SimNet`, `InterpBuffer` and the link clock.\n554\t- `arpg::coop`:\n555\t  - the handshake (with the code), and the directory with host terms and reports;\n556\t  - entering, joining and leaving, and snapshot adoption (keeping the recipient's co-op\n557\t    state);\n558\t  - the standby and its stream, the three-step handoff, promotion, and resync (buffering\n559\t    early streams);\n560\t  - the hero and place streams, and spawn and despawn with complete actors.\n561\t- `Sim::step_net`, the replay's `net` track, and the `coop` tool.\n562\t- **Exit:** the session suites in `coop.rs` pass, and a four-player `coop` run meets §10's\n563\t  mirror-error, pop and bandwidth targets.\n564\t\n565\t**C2: combat and rewards online.**\n566\t- Hit claims with ids, resolved results and the watermark; prediction; victim-side monster\n567\t  hits; `Killed` with its tick, and personal rewards.\n568\t- `PlaceState` and the mechanics, checking §5.5's table mechanic by mechanic.\n569\t- Scaling with the live rescale in every game, and the online rules (§7).\n570\t- **Exit:**\n571\t  - `coop bots=true` with four players clears levels 1-3 and kills the Hollow King on level 4;\n572\t  - the scaling check (§6) is done;\n573\t  - each class of mechanic has a suite.\n574\t\n575\t**C3: the real network.**\n576\t- `pav_net`: matchbox on native and wasm, and the signaling server with rooms (embedded, and the\n577\t  `shardfall-signal` binary).\n578\t- App wiring: the net thread, the wasm pump, flags and settings, the Co-op window, and handshake\n579\t  errors on screen.\n580\t- `scripts/coop-local.sh`.\n581\t- **Exit:**\n582\t  - two native games and a browser tab play together over localhost WebRTC, with screenshots in\n583\t    the PR;\n584\t  - the Windows build compiles and hosts under Wine with a Linux joiner.\n585\t\n586\t**C4: presence.**\n587\t- Party frames, name plates, the map, pings (world and map), emotes (data, wheel, clips), pad and\n588\t  touch bindings, and sound cues.\n589\t- **Exit:** captures show name plates and pings, smoke screenshots show the egui parts, and\n590\t  suites cover delivery.\n591\t\n592\t**C5: hardening and docs.**\n593\t- Hardening:\n594\t  - rejoining within 60 s keeps your slot;\n595\t  - host and owner drops are handled under load;\n596\t  - bandwidth caps;\n597\t  - net lines in `shardfall.log`: connect, RTT, owner changes, resyncs.\n598\t- Docs:\n599\t  - `docs/COOP.md` (new) for players: hosting, joining, a port forward or Tailscale;\n600\t  - `docs/REFERENCE.md`: the tool and the tests;\n601\t  - a pointer in `AGENTS.md`, which must stay under 32 KiB;\n602\t  - a section in `docs/GAME.md`;\n603\t  - `docs/PROGRESS.md`.\n604\t- **Exit:** a 30-minute soak with four bots, one owner drop and one host drop: no stuck place,\n605\t  no panic, and fewer than one resync per minute.\n606\t\n607\t**C6: internet play (after E1).** The public signaling server and optional TURN. The web build\n608\tpoints at the server, the user plays a real session, and their notes become follow-up work.\n609\t\n610\t## 12. Rulings\n611\tThese are asked in chat with this plan (2026-10-16; `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`). Each has a default,\n612\twhich the run applies if no answer has come 15 minutes after it reaches the question.\n613\t\n614\t| # | Question | Default |\n615\t|---|---|---|\n616\t| E1 | Where does a public signaling server run? The account, and any small cost, are the user's. | None yet. Hosts run the embedded server, internet play needs a port forward or a VPN, and browser players wait for the answer (C6). |\n617\t| E2 | Scale by the players in the place, or by everyone in the session (the request's literal \"in the game\")? | In the place, so a player fighting alone doesn't face four players' monsters. |\n618\t| E3 | Personal loot, or one shared drop to fight over? | Personal: each player gets single player's drops. |\n619\t\n620\t## 13. Risks\n621\t- **webrtc-rs on Windows (mingw):** `ring` builds C and assembly. Verify this first in C3; if it\n622\t  won't build, escalate rather than drop the browser.\n623\t- **Strict NATs:** some pairs of players won't connect without TURN (C6).\n624\t- **Snapshot size grows with content:** measured at 41-175 KB; budget 300 KB, and leave caches\n625\t  out with `#[serde(skip)]`.\n626\t- **Big snapshots on the browser's main thread:** decoding about 1.5 MB of CBOR may hitch a\n627\t  frame. Measure in C3, and spread the work over frames if needed.\n628\t- **Promotion after a drop is approximate:** brains restart, and up to 5 s of far-off changes\n629\t  can be lost. That's acceptable for a crash; graceful leaves hand over exactly.\n630\t- **The MCP tool list is full:** keep to one `coop` tool with short help, and trim elsewhere if\n631\t  `asset_errors.rs` fails.\n632\t- **The bot's known stall** on level 4's seal (`docs/PROGRESS.md`, \"Open issues\") may block C2's\n633\t  exit; fix it there if it does.\n634\t\n635\t## 14. Running it\n636\t**On hold:** use this section only once the user takes co-op off hold (see the status at the\n637\ttop).\n638\t\n639\tThe expected size is about 8,000-12,000 lines over six PRs (C0-C5); C6 waits on E1.\n640\t1. **Before you start:** answer E1-E3 (§12) if you can, so the run doesn't stop on them.\n641\t2. **Start fresh, don't compact.** Open a new session, or `/clear` this one, then give the\n642\t   `/goal` below. This file carries everything the run needs, and a `/compact` summary of\n643\t   earlier work would add tokens that every later call re-reads. A new cloud session builds\n644\t   `pav` in its start hook.\n645\t3. **Effort: extra (xhigh).** The design decisions are made here; what remains is a long,\n646\t   careful engineering run with tool-checked exits. That suits xhigh. Max would mostly add cost,\n647\t   and medium or high would risk cutting corners on ownership and resync edge cases.\n648\t4. **The goal line:**\n649\t\n650\t```\n651\t/goal Build co-op as docs/COOP_PLAN.md specifies: stages C0-C5 in order, one PR per stage merged when its checks pass, single player unchanged. Done when §10's targets hold in a four-player bot session and two native games and a browser tab play together over localhost WebRTC.\n652\t```\n653\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33b9a197-b2fe-42e5-9a43-b32ae2dde858","uuid":"6084adeb-adcf-44a6-bebf-08f29a5b31ff","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:03.543Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/.scout/shardfall/docs/COOP_PLAN.md","content":"# Co-op: up to four players, one world\n\nThe plan for online co-op in Shardfall: up to four players, each free to go anywhere, playing\nthe same game as single player, with monsters made tougher instead of more numerous.\n\n**Status: on hold** (the user's call, 2026-10-17). The plan is kept for later, not scrapped;\nnothing is built. Don't start any stage, or run §14's `/goal`, until the user takes it off hold.\nWhen it resumes, re-check §3's facts against `main` first, since the code will have moved.\n\nFacts come from a survey of `main` at `dd5584b`; line numbers are as of then, and *unverified*\nmarks what was not checked. The agent who builds it reads this whole file, then works through\n§11's stages in order. Paths without a crate are under `crates/pav_core/src/`. Players get their\nown page, `docs/COOP.md`, in C5.\n\n## 1. What the user asked\n| The ask | Where this plan meets it |\n|---|---|\n| **Up to four players**, online co-op, \"essentially the same single player experience\" | §2 (1-5), §5.1 (the cap), §5.5, §7 |\n| **No extra monsters:** monster **life and damage scale** with the number of players | §6 |\n| **Lag-free feel:** whatever it takes on every side (host, client, server) | §2 (2-4, 8-9), §5.7, §9, with measured targets in §10 |\n| **No anti-cheat:** every client is trusted | §2 (4), §5.6: claims are never re-checked |\n| **Free roaming:** anyone goes anywhere they can reach on their own, never tied to another player, never waiting (\"a super mini-MMO\") | §2 (1-2), §5.2: one shared instance per place, with travel gated only by the hero's own progress |\n| **No voice or text chat.** Emotes with animations, pings and map pings are in | §8 |\n| A short `/goal` to build it, the effort level, and whether to `/compact` or `/goal` first | §14 |\n| Implied by `AGENTS.md`: the Windows `.exe`, Linux and the browser build play together | §9, §11 (C3) |\n\n## 2. The design in one page\n1. **One game, one place, as today.** Each player's game simulates only the place its hero is\n   in (town, a level, the arena, the lab). One `Sim` holds one place, and travel rebuilds it.\n2. **Place owners.** The first player into a place builds it as single player does and **owns**\n   it: their game runs its monsters, bosses and shared mechanics. Players who arrive later are\n   **guests** whose games mirror the owner's. One guest is the place's **standby**. When the\n   owner leaves, the standby takes over from an exact snapshot, confirmed before the owner goes;\n   if the owner drops, the standby promotes from a snapshot at most 5 s old. A player alone in a\n   place is playing single player, with zero lag.\n3. **Your hero is yours.** Every game fully simulates its own hero: movement, skills, life,\n   mana, potions, items, death. Input never waits on the network.\n4. **The attacker decides it hit; the victim decides it was hit.** Your hits resolve on your\n   screen against what you see and go to the owner as claims. A monster's attack resolves on\n   its target's screen against where that hero really is. Nothing is re-checked (§5.6).\n5. **Personal rewards, shared world.** Loot, gold, XP, boons, chest payouts, vendors, the stash\n   and waypoints are per player and never cross the network: on a kill, each player nearby\n   rolls their own drops, as in single player. Monsters, bosses, seals and the level's\n   mechanics are shared (§5.5).\n6. **Scaling:** life ×(1 + 0.75(n−1)) and damage ×(1 + 0.25(n−1)), where n is the number of\n   players in the place (ruling E2; §6).\n7. **Joining a place means receiving the owner's exact state.** The owner sends its `SimState`\n   snapshot, the CBOR that `Sim::save_state` already writes, minus its own hero. Measured\n   deflated with gzip: 41 KB for town, 81 KB for level 3, 109 KB for level 9 and 175 KB for\n   level 14. Machines therefore never have to compute identical results.\n8. **One replication pattern.** Every character simulated on another machine is a **movement\n   stream** (interpolated) plus **action events** (casts, dodges, emotes, deaths), played\n   through the engine's own animation and skills.\n9. **Transport:** WebRTC data channels through matchbox, on native and in the browser: a full\n   mesh with an unreliable channel for streams and reliable channels for control, events and\n   bulk transfers (§5.4). Tests and tools use an in-process simulated network. The session host\n   keeps only the directory of who owns which place, and hands it on under a new term if it\n   leaves (§5.1).\n10. **Online rules:** no pause, rewind or speed control. Hit-stop freezes only your hero and\n    what you hit, never the world.\n\n**Rejected:**\n- *One host simulating every place* (host-authoritative). Every guest would feel latency on\n  every monster, even when alone, and the host's game would run up to five places at once.\n- *Lockstep or rollback* (for example GGRS). Both need bit-identical simulation on Windows,\n  Linux and wasm, which isn't true here: rapier's `enhanced-determinism` is off and the code\n  uses `std` trig. Every player would also simulate every place.\n- *A WebSocket relay.* On TCP, one lost packet stalls everything behind it, and every message\n  takes two hops.\n\n## 3. What the code assumes today\n| Area | Today | Where | Co-op change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| The player | `SimState.player`; one `InputFrame` per tick, given only to the player | `sim.rs` `spawn_player` l.412, `step_inner` l.521 (input l.553) | Stays the local hero. Remote heroes use a new remote-drive mode (C0). |\n| Shardfall's hero | `Game.hero` (the profile) and `hero_id`; live life and mana are in `Actor` | `arpg/mod.rs` l.123-126, `resume_game` l.324 | `Game` splits into a hero side and a place side (C0). |\n| Monster AI | `Game::pre` takes one hero: wake at 12 m, pack at 9 m, casts at the hero's feet; one flow field cached per hero cell | `arpg/mod.rs` `pre` l.629-729, `arpg/brain.rs` l.11-12 and `think` l.56, `arpg/mechanics.rs` `hero_flow` l.248 | Target the nearest living hero (sticky); one flow field per targeted hero. |\n| Engine checks | one `player_feet` for every engine brain; `PLAYER_CRUMBLE` tiles react only to the player | `sim.rs` l.545, `ai.rs`, `destruct.rs` l.78 | Any hero (the Crumbling Halls need it). |\n| Time | hit-stop slows the whole sim to 0.06 | `Game::time_scale`, `arpg/mod.rs` l.271; `sim.rs` l.524 | Per entity online (§7). |\n| Pause, rewind, speed | the app's `TimeControl`; the Esc menu pauses; Backspace rewinds | `pav_app/src/simhost.rs` l.20-38; `app.rs` l.764, l.1000 | Off online. |\n| Travel | rebuilds the whole `SimState` within one tick, carrying the `Game`; `resume_game` wipes actors, loot and the level; the layout seed comes from the sim's RNG, so a level is new on every visit | `arpg/mod.rs` `game_travel` l.404; `arpg/world.rs` l.513 | Kept. Co-op decides whether to build fresh (owner) or adopt the owner's snapshot (guest). |\n| Damage | `Game::hit` for everything; `targets()` filters by `Team::hostile`, so there is no friendly fire | `arpg/mod.rs` l.1077; `arpg/skills.rs` `fire_cast` l.455, `targets` l.416 | One routing check at the top of `Game::hit` (§5.6). |\n| Rewards | `kill()` gives XP to the one `g.hero`; `drop_loot` uses that hero's find stats | `arpg/mod.rs` l.1309; `arpg/loot.rs` l.50 | Each game runs its own reward on a `Killed` event. |\n| Scaling hooks | `spawn_actor` sets life (l.598) and damage (l.611); `enemy_damage` is also applied in `roll_damage`, the boss's `death_burst` and powers | `arpg/mod.rs` l.578; `arpg/skills.rs` l.331; `arpg/boss.rs` l.350; `arpg/powers.rs` l.251, l.348 | Life scales on the owner, damage on the victim (§6). |\n| Where you can go | `GameCmd::Travel` is gated by the hero's own `max_depth`; `use_exit` refuses while the level is sealed | `arpg/cmd.rs` l.296; `arpg/mechanics.rs` l.911 | Unchanged: \"anywhere they can reach on their own\". |\n| HUD and map | the map takes the hero to be the first `Team::Hero` actor | `pav_app/src/arpg_ui.rs` `level_hud` l.562 (l.641) | Use `hero_id`; draw remote heroes and pings. |\n| View | `RenderFrame.player` and `focus` are single; every entity carries its own puppet look | `sim.rs` `frame` l.941; `pav_view/src/build.rs` l.1104 | Unchanged (the local hero); four heroes already render. |\n| Saves | one hero and roster per machine or browser | `pav_app/src/save.rs` | Unchanged: no save crosses the network. |\n| Bots and tools | `autoplay` and `campaign` drive `g.hero` in one `Session` | `arpg/bot.rs`; `pav_tools/src/agent_tools.rs` l.227 | Per player, through the `coop` tool (§10). |\n| App | no title screen (boots into `town`); the pause menu is the only menu | `pav_app/src/settings.rs` l.172; `ui.rs` `MenuAction` l.25, `pause_menu` l.167 | The co-op entries go in the pause menu. |\n| Networking | none; no async runtime in use (tokio is only in `Cargo.lock`) | every `Cargo.toml` | A new crate, `pav_net` (§4). |\n| Several sims | already work in one process (travel builds a second `Sim`); content caches are process-global | `arpg/mod.rs` l.409 | Enough for the in-process test network. |\n\n## 4. Where the code goes\n- **`pav_core::net`** (new; engine API):\n  - `PeerId`, `NetId` and `Channel` (unreliable or reliable).\n  - A tick's `NetInbox` and `NetOutbox`.\n  - `InterpBuffer`: interpolates, extrapolates up to a limit, then holds.\n  - A link clock: RTT, jitter and the remote-tick estimate.\n  - `SimNet`: an in-process network whose links have latency, jitter, loss and bandwidth drawn\n    from a seed.\n\n  It is pure: no IO, no threads, safe on wasm. It is written for any game on the hook; Shardfall\n  uses it first.\n- **`pav_core::arpg::coop`** (new; the game): messages, the directory, owner and guest logic,\n  the hero and place streams, damage routing, scaling, pings and emotes. Its state is\n  `Game.net: Option<Box<CoopState>>`, inside `SimState`, so a player's own snapshots and\n  replays carry it. It has two parts:\n  - the **session part** (directory, peers, host term, link clocks) lasts the whole session;\n  - the **place part** (role, place epoch, `NetId` map, interpolation buffers, pending events\n    and claims, watermarks) belongs to one place.\n\n  Place snapshots sent to other players carry neither part. Adopting a snapshot keeps the\n  recipient's session part and builds a fresh place part from the snapshot; so does any travel\n  (§5.2).\n- **`pav_net`** (new crate; internals, doctrine 8): the WebRTC transport (matchbox, on native and\n  wasm) and the signaling server with rooms (native only; §9), both embedded in the host's game\n  and as a small `shardfall-signal` binary. It handles bytes only and knows nothing of the game,\n  so `pav_core` stays free of IO.\n- **`pav_app`**: flags and settings, the net pump, the Co-op window, party frames, ping and emote\n  input and wheels, and pad and touch bindings.\n- **`pav_view`**: name plates and ping markers, drawn in the 3D scene so captures show them.\n- **`pav_tools`**: one `coop` tool. The MCP tool list is at its 48,000-character cap\n  (`pav_tools/tests/asset_errors.rs` l.101).\n- **Encoding:**\n  - Messages use `postcard` 1.1.3: compact serde, a new dependency.\n  - Snapshots use the existing CBOR (`Sim::save_state`, `ciborium`), deflated with\n    `miniz_oxide` 0.8, already a workspace dependency.\n  - A malformed message is dropped and logged, never a panic.\n\n## 5. How it works\n### 5.1 Session and directory\n- A session is a matchbox full-mesh room at `<signal>/<code>`. The code is six characters of\n  Crockford base32. Every player links to every other: at most six links. Rooms are kept apart\n  by our signaling server (§9), and `Hello` carries the code as a second check.\n- **Handshake:** a joining player sends `Hello{protocol, build, code, name, profile}`. The host\n  answers `Welcome{slot, directory, settings}` or `Reject{reason}`:\n  - The reason is a version mismatch (the `.exe` and the browser build can differ; bump\n    `PROTOCOL` whenever a message changes), a wrong code, or a full session (a fifth player).\n  - Failures show on screen in plain words: host not found, version mismatch, session full.\n- **The host** is the room's creator. It keeps the **directory**:\n  - peers: slot 1-4, colour, name, and a hero profile (character, level, look);\n  - places: `PlaceKey` → owner, standby, members, epoch (the standby is the member who takes\n    over if the owner drops; §5.2);\n  - the session settings: the host's difficulty sliders and the `coop.*` params.\n\n  It broadcasts every change, and every player keeps a copy.\n- **If the host leaves or drops,** copies of the directory may differ (a broadcast can stop\n  partway), so the new host rebuilds it rather than trusting its copy:\n  - The earliest-joined connected player (ties by peer id) becomes host under a new **host\n    term**: the pair (number, host's peer id).\n    - Every control message carries the term, and messages from older terms are dropped.\n    - If a split link ever leaves two hosts, the higher pair wins; the other host steps down\n      and reports.\n  - The new host asks every player for `Report{place, role, place epoch, name, profile,\n    settings version}` and rebuilds the directory from the answers:\n    - if two players claim to own one place, the higher place epoch wins and the other resyncs\n      as a guest;\n    - the settings with the highest version win.\n  - It publishes the rebuilt directory before accepting any change.\n\n  With the embedded signaling server (E1's default), nobody new can join after the host goes,\n  but the session goes on.\n- **`PlaceKey`** is one of `Town`, `Arena`, `Lab` or `Level(depth)`. There is one instance per\n  key per session: whoever goes to depth 7 joins whoever is already there.\n\n### 5.2 Entering, leaving and owning a place\n**Entering.** Travel starts as today: the portal window, `T`, or the exit, through\n`GameCmd::Travel`, gated by the hero's own `max_depth`. Then:\n1. The game sends `Enter(place)` to the host and keeps its loading veil up.\n2. **Empty place:** the host answers `Assign{owner: you, epoch}`. The game builds the place as\n   single player does, and owns it.\n3. **Occupied place:** the host answers `Assign{owner: O}`. The game asks O to `Join`.\n   - O sends `PlaceSnapshot`: its `SimState` minus its hero side and its `Game.net`, deflated,\n     in 16 KB chunks on the bulk channel (about 0.1-0.5 s).\n   - The game **adopts** it: it keeps its own hero side and co-op state, turns O and the other\n     members into remote heroes, and spawns its hero at the place's spawn. It is now a guest.\n4. O adds the newcomer's hero, and every member gets `MemberJoined`.\n\n**Leaving** is travelling away or disconnecting:\n- **A guest leaves:** the owner removes its hero, and members get `MemberLeft`.\n- **Every place with guests has a standby:** its earliest-joined guest, named in the directory;\n  if the standby leaves, the next guest takes the role. The owner keeps the standby promotable:\n  - it sends the standby the place stream without the interest limit (far actors at 2 Hz);\n  - it sends a full snapshot every 5 s, or every 10 s once snapshots pass 100 KB;\n  - this owner-to-standby link has its own budget of 40 KB/s, on top of §5.4's per-player\n    budget.\n- **The owner leaves, others remain:** the standby takes over in three steps, so no change is\n  lost:\n  1. The old owner takes a final snapshot and sends it as `Handoff`. From that tick it stops\n     advancing the place: monsters hold, and claims and uses queue unapplied. So nothing\n     happens after the snapshot that the snapshot lacks.\n  2. The standby adopts it and answers `Ready`.\n  3. The old owner tells the host, which broadcasts `OwnerChanged{epoch+1}` to re-point\n     everyone's streams. The old owner forwards its queued claims and uses to the new owner,\n     then travels behind its loading veil, and forwards any claim that still reaches it.\n\n  Monsters pause for a fraction of a second at a handoff.\n\n  If `Ready` hasn't come within 3 s, the old owner travels anyway and the host treats it as a\n  drop.\n- **The owner drops:** after `owner_timeout` (2 s) with no packets, the host makes the standby\n  the owner. The standby **promotes**: it adopts its latest standby snapshot, applies its fresher\n  stream data on top, and switches the mirrors to full AI. Brains restart, and up to 5 s of\n  far-off changes can be lost: acceptable for a crash.\n- **The last player leaves:** the place is gone. The next visitor builds a new one, as in single\n  player.\n\n**Epochs** drop stale messages from a former owner. If two players enter an empty place\ntogether, the host answers in order and the second waits for the first's snapshot.\n\n**Resync** heals any desync. A guest asks for a fresh snapshot and adopts it, keeping its hero,\nwhen any of these happens:\n- a `NetId` named by a stream or an event still has no `Spawned` 2 s after it first appeared.\n  A stream, or a `Cast` on the unordered event channel, can beat its spawn; until then it is\n  buffered, not treated as a desync;\n- a `PlaceState` hash doesn't match;\n- an epoch has a gap.\n\nOne adopt function serves joining, handoff, promotion and resync.\n\n**IDs:** the owner assigns each actor's `NetId`, and each game maps `NetId`s to its own\n`EntityId`s, because games allocate entities independently after they adopt a snapshot. Remote\nheroes are `NetId`s by slot.\n\n### 5.3 One tick\n1. The app receives packets and stamps each with the next local tick, giving the `NetInbox`.\n2. `Sim::step_net(&InputFrame, &NetInbox)` runs three steps:\n   - the co-op pre-step applies the inbox: directory, snapshots, events and claims, and\n     streams into the interpolation buffers;\n   - the normal step;\n   - the co-op post-step writes the `NetOutbox`: streams and events.\n3. The app sends the outbox.\n\nReplays record the inbox per tick in a new `net` track in `Replay`. So `record_save` and\n`replay` still reproduce one player's game exactly on the machine that recorded it (doctrine 5;\n`AGENTS.md`'s platform note). Single player calls `step_net` with an empty inbox and behaves\nexactly as today.\n\n### 5.4 Streams and events\nFour channels, so a lost or large message never holds up an unrelated one:\n- **0 streams:** unreliable and unordered, each message at most 1,200 bytes.\n- **1 control:** reliable and ordered: the handshake, the directory, owner changes and the\n  handoff's steps.\n- **2 events:** reliable and unordered. Each event carries a per-sender sequence number, and\n  order is restored only where it matters:\n  - a `NetId`'s spawn, death and despawn are kept as tombstones, so a late spawn never revives\n    a dead actor;\n  - each player's claims apply in id order (§5.6).\n- **3 bulk:** reliable and ordered: snapshots and `PlaceState`.\n\n| Message | Ch. | When | From → to | Carries |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Hero stream | 0 | 30 Hz | each player → others in its place | tick, position, velocity, facing, grounded and posture, life and mana fractions, ailment bits, current action and phase |\n| Place stream | 0 | 20 Hz | owner → each guest | owner tick and, per player, the last claim applied (the claim watermark); then per actor in the guest's interest: `NetId`, position, velocity, yaw, life fraction, state bits (casting, stunned, frozen, burning, dead), target |\n| Standby stream, standby snapshot | 0, 3 | far actors 2 Hz; snapshot every 5-10 s | owner → the standby | the place stream without the interest limit; a full snapshot (§5.2; on its own 40 KB/s budget) |\n| `Cast`, `Dodge` | 2 | on event | whoever runs the character → its place | actor, skill, aim, target, start tick, seed |\n| `Spawned`, `Despawned` | 2 | on event | owner → guests | `NetId`, and the new entity and its `Actor` as in the owner's state (CBOR), so summons, waves and brood arrive complete. Every reference inside them to another entity (`Actor.master`, `last_hit`, hit lists, owners, joints) travels as a `NetId` and is remapped on receipt; an unknown one becomes `None` |\n| `Killed` | 2 | on event | owner → guests | `NetId`, owner tick, killer, position, level, rarity, rank, boss |\n| `HitClaim` | 2 | per hit | guest → owner | claim id (rising, per claimant), target, and the attacker's side as its game resolved it: damage by type before the target's mitigation, crit, ailments with strength and duration, knockback, execute, power effects |\n| `UseClaim` | 2 | per use | guest → owner | a world-changing use: a chest, a well, a keg, a Menagerie creature, an arena wave |\n| `HeroHit`, `HeroDied`, `Respawned` | 2 | on event | a player → its place | others' screens: flash, number, fall |\n| `PlaceState` | 3 | on change, ≤ 5 Hz | owner → guests | the shared mechanics state, deflated and sequence-numbered (latest wins) |\n| `PartyStatus` | 2 | 1 Hz | a player → all | life, level, place, dead (for party frames) |\n| `ProfileChanged` | 2 | on change | a player → all | character, level, look |\n| `Emote`, `Ping` | 2 | on use | a player → its place | §8 |\n| `Enter`, `Assign`, `Join`, `Handoff`, `Ready`, `OwnerChanged`, `Report` | 1 (`Handoff` on 3) | on change | the players concerned | the host term and place epoch, plus each step's data (§5.1, §5.2) |\n| `Probe`, `Echo` | 0 | 1 Hz per link | both ways | the link clock |\n\nThe place stream covers actors in the guest's interest: within 45 m of its hero, every boss,\nand anything targeting it. It sends the nearest first and goes round-robin past the size cap.\n\nBudget: at most 40 KB/s per player with four players in one level, not counting the standby's\nlink, which has its own budget (§5.2). Positions start as f32; quantize them only if a\nmeasurement goes over budget.\n\n### 5.5 Who simulates what\n| Thing | Simulated by | Other games |\n|---|---|---|\n| Each hero | its player's game, fully | remote-drive from the hero stream; casts, dodges and emotes from events; its damage ignored |\n| Monsters, bosses, summons, chest keepers, arena waves, Menagerie creatures | the place's owner (AI, life, ailments, deaths) | mirrors: remote-drive from the place stream; casts from events |\n| Projectiles and ground effects | every game that plays the cast | damage only as §5.6 routes it |\n| Shared mechanics: kegs, rift gates, wells, totems, the crumbling floor, the seal and exit, chests and their waves | the owner | `PlaceState` and events (a keg's blast: each game applies it to its own hero) |\n| Static hazards: lava, spike beats, wind, ice, darkness, time bubbles | every game, on its own hero (the owner also on monsters) | not sent |\n| Personal: loot, gold, XP, potions, shrine boons, chest payouts, the passive point for a first boss clear, vendor and buyback, the stash, waypoints (`max_depth`), fog of war (`LevelState.seen`), the kill streak | each player's game only | never sent |\n| Cosmetic: villagers, Biscuit, the Hall of Heroes performers, floaters, gibs, particles, shake | each game locally | never sent |\n\nShrines are personal: each player can use each shrine once. A guest's world-changing use plays\nat once in its own game and goes to the owner as a `UseClaim`.\n\n*Unverified:* how time bubbles and wind act on characters. In C2, check each mechanic in\n`arpg/mechanics.rs` against this table, and record any changes in the stage's `docs/HISTORY.md`\nentry.\n\n### 5.6 Damage routing\nOne check at the top of `Game::hit` returns `Apply`, `Claim` or `Ignore`:\n\n| Target ↓ \\ attacker → | your hero | a monster or hazard | a remote hero |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| your hero | — (no friendly fire) | **Apply**: you decide you were hit | — |\n| a monster or object, in a place you own | **Apply** | **Apply** | **Ignore**: its player claims |\n| a monster or object, in a place where you're a guest | **Claim**: play it now, send `HitClaim` | **Ignore** | **Ignore** |\n| a remote hero | — | **Ignore**: its player decides | — |\n\nThe owner applies a claim through `Game::hit`:\n- **The attacker's side is used as sent:** no re-roll, and no reading of the attacker's powers\n  or build, which the owner doesn't have.\n- **The owner applies its own current defences for the target:** armour, resistances, wards\n  and buffs. The guest predicted with its mirror's defences; the watermark corrects any\n  difference.\n- **Order:** it applies each player's claims in id order, holding any that arrive early. The\n  watermark it advertises is the last claim applied, so it never passes a gap.\n- It credits the claimer.\n\nEffects on the attacker (life and mana on hit, power procs, hit-stop) happen in the attacker's\ngame when the hit lands there. Single player is the \"a place you own\" row.\n\n### 5.7 Lag hiding\n1. **Your own hero has no latency** (§2, point 3).\n2. **Other players' characters show at the owner's time minus an interpolation delay:** 1.5\n   times the stream interval plus the p95 jitter, clamped to 50-200 ms. On loss they\n   extrapolate for up to 150 ms, then hold. Corrections blend over 100 ms; teleports (rift\n   gates, respawn) snap.\n3. **Hits are instant on your screen.** The flash, number and sound, your hit-stop and your\n   life on hit all happen at once. The monster's bar shows predicted life: the owner's latest\n   figure minus your claims above the watermark that came with it, so no claim is subtracted\n   twice. A predicted kill plays the death and drops your loot at once, since loot is personal.\n   If the watermark hasn't passed the killing claim within 1 s, the mirror comes back (rare).\n4. **You dodge what you see.** Monster attacks resolve on your machine against your hero, with\n   the full windup. It is never shortened, which is fair to the victim.\n5. **Interactions** (rift gate, well, keg, chest) play at once in your game; the owner confirms.\n6. **The link clock** sends a probe on each link every second and keeps RTT, jitter (EWMA) and\n   each place's owner-tick estimate.\n7. **Heroes pass through each other**; monsters still collide with heroes.\n8. **The owner is whoever entered first.** If phones or slow browsers owning a place make\n   guests' monsters stutter, hand ownership to a native player in the place. Build this only if\n   it shows up.\n\n## 6. Scaling\n- **n** is the number of players in the place (E2's default). Setting `coop.scale_by = \"session\"`\n  counts everyone in the session instead.\n- **Life** is multiplied by 1 + `coop.life_per_player` × (n−1). The default 0.75 gives ×1,\n  ×1.75, ×2.5 and ×3.25.\n  - The owner applies it in `spawn_actor` (`arpg/mod.rs` l.598).\n  - When n changes, every game rescales the actors it holds in the same way, keeping each\n    life fraction (`Actor::recompute`, `arpg/combat.rs` l.295): the owner its monsters, guests\n    their mirrors. So predictions, execute thresholds and damage numbers use the owner's\n    maximum.\n- **Damage** is multiplied by 1 + `coop.damage_per_player` × (n−1). The default 0.25 gives ×1,\n  ×1.25, ×1.5 and ×1.75.\n  - It is applied where monster damage is rolled in the victim's game, using that game's n,\n    next to `enemy_damage`.\n  - The places are `roll_damage` (`arpg/skills.rs` l.331), `death_burst` (`arpg/boss.rs`\n    l.350) and powers (`arpg/powers.rs` l.251, l.348).\n- **Rewards** are per player, as single player gives them. On `Killed`, every player within\n  `coop.share_radius` (40 m) of the kill, or who hit the monster, gets the full XP and rolls\n  their own drops. Distance is judged at the kill's owner tick: each game keeps its hero's\n  positions for the last 2 s, and judges an older kill from its oldest kept position.\n- Ward Totems and kegs keep their own life, as today.\n- The session host's difficulty sliders apply in every game.\n- All four params are a `Tunable` (`coop.*`), so `params`, `set`, presets and the tuning panel\n  get them.\n- **Check (C2):** compare four bots in a place against one bot alone. If kill time or deaths\n  differ by more than 25%, adjust the defaults once and record it. Feel is the user's call.\n\n## 7. Online changes to single-player features\n- **Pause, speed, single step and rewind** (the Esc menu, F6, Backspace, the pad's Select): off in\n  a session. The Esc menu opens over the running game.\n- **Hit-stop:** `Game::time_scale` returns 1 in a session. Your hero freezes for the hit-stop's\n  length, and the struck monster's pose holds on your screen. The boss entrance's 0.35 s freeze\n  becomes a camera beat.\n- **Studios, the F10 editor and the engine demo scenes** (world, rooms, starter): leave the\n  session first. Co-op covers Shardfall's places only.\n- **Character switching** in the Hall of Heroes: allowed in town; it sends `ProfileChanged`.\n- **Death:** as single player: 3 s, then respawn at the place's spawn with full life. Other\n  players see you fall and reappear.\n- **Saves:** each game saves its own hero, as now. Rewind history isn't recorded online.\n\n## 8. Pings, emotes and the co-op UI\n**Pings.** The keys below are free in the game today (`pav_app/src/input.rs` l.111-129,\n`app.rs` l.835-944).\n- **World pings:** `V` (or mouse Back) pings the ground under the cursor, and the context picks\n  the kind:\n  - a monster → **Danger**;\n  - an item, feature or spot → **Look**;\n  - your own hero → **Help**;\n  - anything else → **Go**.\n\n  Hold `V` for a wheel of the four. On a pad, R3 pings at the aim point (hold it for the wheel).\n  On touch, a ping button sits in the top-right row (`touch.rs` l.410-442).\n- **Map pings:** with the big map open (`M`), click while holding `V` (or `Alt`) to ping that\n  spot.\n- **What the others see** (players in the same place only):\n  - a ring and beam in the pinger's colour, with the kind's icon, for 4 s, drawn by `pav_view`\n    so captures show them;\n  - an arrow at the screen edge with the distance, when the ping is off screen;\n  - a blip on the minimap and the big map;\n  - a short synth cue per kind (`pav_audio`).\n- **Limit:** at most 3 pings per 3 s per player; extras are dropped.\n\n**Emotes.**\n- **Eight**, defined as data in `game/emotes.toml` (key, name, clip, loop, icon): wave, cheer,\n  bow, dance, laugh, point, sit and taunt.\n  - Clips come from the store (`assets shelf=clip`, `assets find=<word> kind=clip`), keeping\n    each source's credit and licence: open-licence tiers only, never `anim/local`.\n  - A missing clip is made through the Animation Studio's loop.\n- **Use:** hold `B` for the emote wheel (pad: L3; touch: an emote button). The emote plays on\n  your hero at once and stops when you move, attack or dodge. Other players' games play it on\n  your hero too.\n- *Unverified:* that every clip plays on all three heroes' puppets. Check with `animsheet` in\n  C4.\n\n**UI** (egui in `pav_app`; the `coop` tool reports the same state as text, because captures\ndon't show egui):\n- **Pause menu → Co-op:**\n  - **Host:** the code, a copy button, and \"players n/4\".\n  - **Join:** a code, or `CODE@ip:port` for a host's embedded server.\n  - **Leave.**\n- **Flags and settings:**\n  - flags: `--host`, `--join CODE[@addr]` and `--name NAME`;\n  - `shardfall.toml`: `name`, `signal = \"wss://...\"`, `signal_port = 3536`, `turn`,\n    `turn_user` and `turn_pass`;\n  - the browser: `?join=CODE`.\n\n  The game still boots into town; hosting turns the running game into a session.\n- **Party frames**, up to three: slot colour, name, character, level, a life bar, the place\n  (\"Emberwatch\", \"Depth 7, The Molten Floor\"), a dead mark, and a link-quality dot.\n- **Name plates** over remote heroes (`pav_view`): name and level in the slot colour, with a thin\n  life bar.\n- **Map:** remote heroes as arrows in their colours, and pings.\n- **Toasts** for joins, leaves and lost connections. The text is fixed; there is no chat.\n- **Slot colours:** 1 amber `#f2a33a`, 2 cyan `#3ac8f2`, 3 lime `#8fe04a`, 4 magenta `#e04ad0`.\n\n## 9. Transport and connection\n- **The libraries:** **matchbox_socket 0.12.0** (2025-05-21) and **matchbox_signaling 0.12.0**.\n  - They are the newest versions at least 12 months old on 2026-10-16 (doctrine 9; 0.14 is\n    current). Licence: MIT or Apache-2.0.\n  - The same API runs on native and wasm: full-mesh WebRTC data channels with\n    `ChannelConfig::unreliable()`, `reliable()` and custom configs (reliable and unordered is\n    `ordered: false` with unlimited retransmits), ICE servers (`RtcIceServerConfig`, Google\n    STUN by default), and a pluggable `SignallerBuilder`.\n  - Native-to-browser play is a stated matchbox feature, *unverified* here until C3.\n- **Rooms:** the signaling library's built-in full mesh (`full_mesh_builder`) puts every\n  connected peer in one mesh. The standalone `matchbox_server` keeps rooms apart by URL path,\n  using its own topology on the signaling library; its newest qualifying release is 0.11.0, and\n  it has no 0.12. `pav_net` reuses that pattern on `matchbox_signaling` 0.12, so the host's\n  embedded server and the public `shardfall-signal` binary run the same code.\n- **Native:**\n  - It uses webrtc-rs 0.13, with `ring` 0.17 and `rustls` and no OpenSSL; matchbox's lockfile\n    lists about 300 crates.\n  - One `net` thread runs a tokio runtime. On it run matchbox's message loop (which reaches\n    tokio through `async-compat`) and, on the host, the embedded signaling server (axum on\n    tokio).\n  - The sim thread drains and fills the socket's channels each tick.\n  - Check the Windows cross-build (`scripts/build-windows.sh`) on the first day of C3.\n- **wasm** uses the browser's own WebRTC. The message loop runs under\n  `wasm_bindgen_futures::spawn_local`, and the socket is polled in `pump()`\n  (`pav_app/src/app.rs` l.990). No threads and no blocking calls.\n- **Signaling (E1):**\n  - By default, the host's native game runs `pav_net`'s signaling server on port 3536.\n    Joiners use `CODE@host:3536` on a LAN, through a port forward, or over a VPN such as\n    Tailscale.\n  - A browser page served over https can only reach a `wss://` server.\n    Internet play from the browser waits on E1.\n  - Recommended: `shardfall-signal` in a small container with TLS, its URL in\n    `shardfall.toml` and the web build.\n- **NAT:** STUN only by default. TURN comes from settings if a real session fails to connect;\n  most home networks connect without it.\n- **Sizes:** unreliable messages are at most 1,200 bytes; snapshots go in 16 KB chunks on the\n  bulk channel.\n\n## 10. Tools, tests and targets\n- **`SimNet`:** N players in one process, each with a full `Sim`, over links with seeded latency,\n  jitter, loss and bandwidth. One tick steps every game and the network. Runs are deterministic,\n  so tests and tools repeat exactly.\n- **The `coop` tool** (one tool, help at most 50 tokens):\n  - `coop players=4 latency=80 jitter=15 loss=0.02` starts an in-process session; player 1 is\n    the current session.\n  - `coop peer=2` points the other tools (`input`, `player`, `capture`, `see`, `game_cmd`,\n    `level`) at player 2's game; `step` advances every game.\n  - `coop` with no arguments reports, for each player: place, role, life, RTT, KB/s,\n    interpolation delay, mirror error, unconfirmed claims and resyncs.\n  - `coop bots=true seconds=60` lets bots drive every player, and reports kills, deaths, XP and\n    clear times with the network numbers.\n  - Through `pav live` it reports the real session.\n- **Targets.** These are objective stand-ins for \"feels lag-free\", measured at 100 ms RTT, ±20 ms\n  jitter and 2% loss, with four players in one level:\n\n  | Measure | Target |\n  |---|---|\n  | Own-hero input latency | 0 ticks (true by construction; asserted) |\n  | Mirror error: a guest's mirror vs the owner's actor at the same owner tick | median ≤ 0.15 m, p99 ≤ 0.5 m |\n  | Pops: corrections over 0.5 m in one frame | ≤ 1 per minute per player |\n  | Bandwidth | ≤ 40 KB/s per player |\n  | Hit confirmation (the watermark passing a claim) | median ≤ RTT + 2 ticks, p99 ≤ 2 × RTT |\n  | A guest's tick cost | ≤ the owner's |\n- **Suites** in `crates/pav_core/tests/coop.rs` (new; cargo, no GPU):\n  - the handshake: version mismatch, a full session;\n  - entering, joining, leaving, handoff, owner drop and host drop;\n  - a graceful handoff loses no change; an owner drop with players far apart promotes the\n    standby without stale actors; a host drop in mid-broadcast converges on one directory;\n  - a place stream that beats its `Spawned` is buffered, not resynced;\n  - the claim watermark: predicted life never subtracts a claim twice, and claims that arrive\n    out of order never move the watermark past a gap;\n  - a handoff in mid-fight loses no claim;\n  - a spawned brood's `master` points at the right actor in every game;\n  - travelling between two occupied places leaves no stale `NetId`;\n  - a guest's kill confirmed by the owner, with the guest's own loot;\n  - victim-side hits: a dodge on the guest's screen counts;\n  - scaling, and the live rescale;\n  - ping and emote delivery;\n  - one player's replay reproducing its hash;\n  - every existing suite, unchanged.\n- **`pav_net` tests:** two native peers and the embedded signaling server on 127.0.0.1 exchange\n  messages on all four channels, and two rooms on one server stay apart.\n- **`scripts/coop-local.sh`** (new), all on localhost, with screenshots from each window into\n  `out/coop/`:\n  - a native host under Xvfb and lavapipe, with `--bridge 127.0.0.1:7878`;\n  - a native joiner on `:7879`;\n  - a browser tab in headless Chromium with `?join=` (the flags are in `docs/REFERENCE.md`,\n    \"Browser and phones\").\n- **Windows:** the `.exe` hosting under Wine (`scripts/smoke-windows.sh`) with a Linux joiner.\n- **Cost:** these are tool checks with no model calls, so they may run as often as needed\n  (`AGENTS.md`, \"Testing budget\"). No agent judges; looks and feel go to the user.\n\n## 11. Stages\nEach stage is one PR, merged when its checks pass (`AGENTS.md`, \"Per PR\"), with a dated\n`docs/HISTORY.md` entry and `docs/PROGRESS.md` brought up to date. Single player must behave\nexactly as before after every stage.\n\n**C0: several heroes in one place (no network).**\n- **Remote-drive mode** for characters (`Character.remote`): a kinematic target, with the\n  animation fed from velocity, posture and grounded. The `riding` early-out (`character.rs`\n  l.494) is the template, but it skips animation, which this mode keeps.\n- **Remote heroes** as `Team::Hero` actors with their own looks. Generalize `refresh_hero`\n  (`arpg/mod.rs` l.441) to any hero profile.\n- **AI for several heroes:** monsters target the nearest living hero, sticky with hysteresis;\n  `hero_flow` is kept per targeted hero; aggro checks every hero. `PLAYER_CRUMBLE` and the\n  other player-only engine checks take any hero.\n- **Split `Game`:**\n  - The hero side is `hero`, `hero_id` and the hero's `Actor`, `loot`, `gold`, `vendor`,\n    `buyback`, `respawn`, `travel`, `floaters`, `message`, `hitstop`, `shake`, `streak`,\n    `echoes`, `power_clock`, `level_flash`, the loot settings, the bags cache and `roster`.\n  - The rest is the place side.\n  - Add `adopt_place(snapshot, hero_side)`, and have travel use it too.\n- **The damage-routing check** (§5.6) with the single-player role, and the `coop.*` params with\n  the scaling functions.\n- **Exit:**\n  - every suite passes unchanged;\n  - `campaign from=1 to=3` matches `main`;\n  - a new test adds a second, remote-driven hero, and monsters go for the nearer one.\n\n**C1: a session over the simulated network.**\n- `pav_core::net`: `SimNet`, `InterpBuffer` and the link clock.\n- `arpg::coop`:\n  - the handshake (with the code), and the directory with host terms and reports;\n  - entering, joining and leaving, and snapshot adoption (keeping the recipient's co-op\n    state);\n  - the standby and its stream, the three-step handoff, promotion, and resync (buffering\n    early streams);\n  - the hero and place streams, and spawn and despawn with complete actors.\n- `Sim::step_net`, the replay's `net` track, and the `coop` tool.\n- **Exit:** the session suites in `coop.rs` pass, and a four-player `coop` run meets §10's\n  mirror-error, pop and bandwidth targets.\n\n**C2: combat and rewards online.**\n- Hit claims with ids, resolved results and the watermark; prediction; victim-side monster\n  hits; `Killed` with its tick, and personal rewards.\n- `PlaceState` and the mechanics, checking §5.5's table mechanic by mechanic.\n- Scaling with the live rescale in every game, and the online rules (§7).\n- **Exit:**\n  - `coop bots=true` with four players clears levels 1-3 and kills the Hollow King on level 4;\n  - the scaling check (§6) is done;\n  - each class of mechanic has a suite.\n\n**C3: the real network.**\n- `pav_net`: matchbox on native and wasm, and the signaling server with rooms (embedded, and the\n  `shardfall-signal` binary).\n- App wiring: the net thread, the wasm pump, flags and settings, the Co-op window, and handshake\n  errors on screen.\n- `scripts/coop-local.sh`.\n- **Exit:**\n  - two native games and a browser tab play together over localhost WebRTC, with screenshots in\n    the PR;\n  - the Windows build compiles and hosts under Wine with a Linux joiner.\n\n**C4: presence.**\n- Party frames, name plates, the map, pings (world and map), emotes (data, wheel, clips), pad and\n  touch bindings, and sound cues.\n- **Exit:** captures show name plates and pings, smoke screenshots show the egui parts, and\n  suites cover delivery.\n\n**C5: hardening and docs.**\n- Hardening:\n  - rejoining within 60 s keeps your slot;\n  - host and owner drops are handled under load;\n  - bandwidth caps;\n  - net lines in `shardfall.log`: connect, RTT, owner changes, resyncs.\n- Docs:\n  - `docs/COOP.md` (new) for players: hosting, joining, a port forward or Tailscale;\n  - `docs/REFERENCE.md`: the tool and the tests;\n  - a pointer in `AGENTS.md`, which must stay under 32 KiB;\n  - a section in `docs/GAME.md`;\n  - `docs/PROGRESS.md`.\n- **Exit:** a 30-minute soak with four bots, one owner drop and one host drop: no stuck place,\n  no panic, and fewer than one resync per minute.\n\n**C6: internet play (after E1).** The public signaling server and optional TURN. The web build\npoints at the server, the user plays a real session, and their notes become follow-up work.\n\n## 12. Rulings\nThese are asked in chat with this plan (2026-10-16; `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`). Each has a default,\nwhich the run applies if no answer has come 15 minutes after it reaches the question.\n\n| # | Question | Default |\n|---|---|---|\n| E1 | Where does a public signaling server run? The account, and any small cost, are the user's. | None yet. Hosts run the embedded server, internet play needs a port forward or a VPN, and browser players wait for the answer (C6). |\n| E2 | Scale by the players in the place, or by everyone in the session (the request's literal \"in the game\")? | In the place, so a player fighting alone doesn't face four players' monsters. |\n| E3 | Personal loot, or one shared drop to fight over? | Personal: each player gets single player's drops. |\n\n## 13. Risks\n- **webrtc-rs on Windows (mingw):** `ring` builds C and assembly. Verify this first in C3; if it\n  won't build, escalate rather than drop the browser.\n- **Strict NATs:** some pairs of players won't connect without TURN (C6).\n- **Snapshot size grows with content:** measured at 41-175 KB; budget 300 KB, and leave caches\n  out with `#[serde(skip)]`.\n- **Big snapshots on the browser's main thread:** decoding about 1.5 MB of CBOR may hitch a\n  frame. Measure in C3, and spread the work over frames if needed.\n- **Promotion after a drop is approximate:** brains restart, and up to 5 s of far-off changes\n  can be lost. That's acceptable for a crash; graceful leaves hand over exactly.\n- **The MCP tool list is full:** keep to one `coop` tool with short help, and trim elsewhere if\n  `asset_errors.rs` fails.\n- **The bot's known stall** on level 4's seal (`docs/PROGRESS.md`, \"Open issues\") may block C2's\n  exit; fix it there if it does.\n\n## 14. Running it\n**On hold:** use this section only once the user takes co-op off hold (see the status at the\ntop).\n\nThe expected size is about 8,000-12,000 lines over six PRs (C0-C5); C6 waits on E1.\n1. **Before you start:** answer E1-E3 (§12) if you can, so the run doesn't stop on them.\n2. **Start fresh, don't compact.** Open a new session, or `/clear` this one, then give the\n   `/goal` below. This file carries everything the run needs, and a `/compact` summary of\n   earlier work would add tokens that every later call re-reads. A new cloud session builds\n   `pav` in its start hook.\n3. **Effort: extra (xhigh).** The design decisions are made here; what remains is a long,\n   careful engineering run with tool-checked exits. That suits xhigh. Max would mostly add cost,\n   and medium or high would risk cutting corners on ownership and resync edge cases.\n4. **The goal line:**\n\n```\n/goal Build co-op as docs/COOP_PLAN.md specifies: stages C0-C5 in order, one PR per stage merged when its checks pass, single player unchanged. Done when §10's targets hold in a four-player bot session and two native games and a browser tab play together over localhost WebRTC.\n```\n","numLines":653,"startLine":1,"totalLines":653}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01S2wxVCcprG5BBGjcwF71yc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj96NzQc31W4h1iMzXhh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015prNaSsyzmX7xtRVYS97XB","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/.scout/shardfall/docs/DESIGN.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5883,"cache_read_input_tokens":59538,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5883},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33b9a197-b2fe-42e5-9a43-b32ae2dde858","uuid":"3d53561b-3b69-465c-bc40-e855bdff0322","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:04.226Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj96Nk25ZgsUr2Lej92v","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_015prNaSsyzmX7xtRVYS97XB":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/.scout/shardfall/docs/DESIGN.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015prNaSsyzmX7xtRVYS97XB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Shardfall — Engine & Showcase Design Plan\n2\t\n3\t\"Shardfall\" is the game and repository name. \"Pavilion\" is the engine's playable tech demo.\n4\tThis document is the source of truth for what we are building and why; the principles are in `docs/DOCTRINE.md`.\n5\tIf a pasted version of this plan in chat differs from this file, the pasted version wins; update this file to match.\n6\t\n7\t## 1. What we're building\n8\tAn AI-first game engine, plus a living tech-demo showcase built on it, written in Rust. Everything you see and hear is generated from code.\n9\t\n10\tThe showcase is a continuous pavilion of themed wings and test rooms set inside an infinite streaming world. It serves as a personal evaluation lab and as living documentation.\n11\t\n12\tThe engine is meant to be reused for many different games. We don't know yet what game we're making, so flexibility wins over polish.\n13\t\n14\t## 2. Principles\n15\t`docs/DOCTRINE.md` sets the principles and the escalation process, and outranks this document.\n16\t`AGENTS.md` says how the doctrine applies to this repo and lists the working rules under it:\n17\tgameplay first, use tools, run well on a modern PC, approximations that feel right, check\n18\tselectively, token efficiency.\n19\t\n20\t## 3. Platform & tech\n21\t- **Where it runs:**\n22\t  - Native desktop app.\n23\t  - The human plays on Windows 11 (RTX 3060 Ti at work, RTX 4070 Super at home).\n24\t  - Agents build, run and test on Linux (cloud containers, WSL2 Ubuntu).\n25\t- **Language:** Rust. The stable toolchain is pinned with `rust-toolchain.toml`, and `Cargo.lock` is committed.\n26\t- **Graphics:** wgpu, which implements the WebGPU API.\n27\t  - Vulkan is the default backend on Windows and Linux.\n28\t  - DX12 can be selected with a startup setting.\n29\t  - No OpenGL or WebGL.\n30\t- **Headless rendering for agents:** Mesa lavapipe (software Vulkan) on machines without a GPU. Same engine code, different driver.\n31\t- **Physics:** rapier3d. Coordinates are f32, with a one-line switch to f64 (`rapier3d-f64`) if we ever see jitter far from the origin.\n32\t- **Other libraries:** winit for window and input, gilrs for gamepads, cpal for audio output (with our own synthesizer), egui for the debug UI.\n33\t- **Browser build:** a late milestone. The same code compiles to WebAssembly and WebGPU. Library choices keep this possible, but we don't maintain it until then.\n34\t- **Assets:** no image, model, texture or audio files. Geometry, surfaces, effects and sound are all generated from code. Fonts are allowed, for UI and in-world text.\n35\t\n36\t## 4. World & camera\n37\t- **The world is fully 3D.** \"2D top-down\" is a camera preset, not an engine limit.\n38\t- **Camera parameters are live:**\n39\t  - tilt, from 90° (straight down) to 0° (side view);\n40\t  - rotation;\n41\t  - distance/zoom;\n42\t  - orthographic or perspective projection;\n43\t  - field of view.\n44\t\n45\t  They can be changed mid-game from the tuning panel, each room saves its own defaults, and game code can animate the camera.\n46\t- **Standard camera helpers:**\n47\t  - fade or cut away geometry between the camera and the player;\n48\t  - hide upper floors while the player is below them;\n49\t  - mouse aim targets the point on the ground plane at the player's height;\n50\t  - movement input is relative to the camera's rotation.\n51\t- **Default look:** tilted to about 60–75°, stylized with flat or cel shading and outlines. A 90° orthographic view gives the clean 2D look.\n52\t\n53\t## 5. Rendering\n54\t- **Pipeline:** a real 3D renderer with depth, MSAA, HDR and a post-processing chain.\n55\t- **Procedural geometry:** boxes, rounded boxes, cylinders, spheres, capsules, extruded shapes, tile terrain, tubes and ropes.\n56\t- **Analytic SDF primitives** (spheres, capsules, etc.) are pixel-perfect at any zoom, and can blend smoothly into each other for organic shapes.\n57\t- **Swappable styles** per room or per object: flat 2D look, cel/toon, or fully lit.\n58\t- **Lighting:** dynamic lights with real shadows, emissive surfaces and bloom.\n59\t- **GPU compute is cosmetic only** (particles and similar). Anything that affects gameplay runs on the CPU.\n60\t- **Text:** in-world text is rendered by the engine from a font; UI text goes through egui.\n61\t\n62\t## 6. Characters\n63\t- **Puppet model:** an invisible skeleton, driven by procedural animation, with parts attached to the bones.\n64\t  - **Procedural animation:** walk cycles driven by speed, foot IK to the ground, leaning into turns, recoil, squash-and-stretch, and secondary motion.\n65\t  - **Parts:** mostly spheres and capsules, so silhouettes read as clean 2D shapes from any camera angle.\n66\t- **One motion, many looks:**\n67\t  - a cutout (flat 2D art on a camera-facing card, for fixed-camera designs);\n68\t  - a puppet with a flat 2D look;\n69\t  - a lit puppet.\n70\t- **Camera-aware rules** recover 2D \"cheats\", such as eyes that stay visible or a body that leans toward the viewer. Stepped animation (\"on twos\") is optional.\n71\t- **Everything is a parameter:** proportions, colors and limb count are sliders.\n72\t\n73\t## 7. Physics, height & movement\n74\t- **Real verticality:**\n75\t  - jumping gaps, climbing onto boxes, ladders, ledges and falls;\n76\t  - crawling under things and ducking projectiles;\n77\t  - multi-floor buildings with destructible floor tiles (blow a hole, drop to the floor below).\n78\t- **Flat rooms** lock motion to a plane (bullet hell). **Side-view rooms** are a camera setting plus an axis lock, so Celeste-style rooms are possible.\n79\t- **High-count projectiles and bullets** use a lightweight custom system, not physics bodies.\n80\t- **Movement models** plug in per character, can be switched from a dropdown per room, and every number in them is tunable:\n81\t  - **Instant:** immediate response, with a hold-to-slow focus button.\n82\t  - **Momentum:** acceleration, skidding, air control.\n83\t  - **Grid/tile:** step-by-step movement.\n84\t  - **Committed animations:** moves play out fully once started.\n85\t  - **Vehicle:** grip and drift.\n86\t  - **Flight:** banking and inertia.\n87\t  - **Special states:** climbing and swimming.\n88\t\n89\t  Each room marks the model it was designed for. Other models can be tried wherever it makes sense.\n90\t- **Tick rate** defaults to 60 per second, with 120 and 240 selectable. Input delay is measurable, and vsync and smoothing can be toggled.\n91\t\n92\t## 8. Simulation architecture\n93\t- **Headless-first:** the simulation core is a library with no window, graphics or audio dependencies. It runs in two ways:\n94\t  - inside the game, on its own thread; the renderer observes and interpolates, and neither blocks the other;\n95\t  - as a headless command-line program at maximum speed.\n96\t- **Time control:** fixed tick rate, speed control from slow motion to maximum, pause and single-step.\n97\t- **Repeatability (the cheap version):** the same machine, build, seed and recorded inputs give the same result.\n98\t  - It exists to support rewind and bug replays.\n99\t  - It is not guaranteed across machines.\n100\t  - If it ever gets in the way of gameplay or performance, gameplay wins.\n101\t- **Time travel:**\n102\t  - periodic snapshots plus an input log;\n103\t  - rewind restores the nearest snapshot and re-simulates forward;\n104\t  - history is limited by memory (adjustable);\n105\t  - acting after a rewind starts a new timeline;\n106\t  - snapshots can be saved to files.\n107\t- **Streaming:**\n108\t  - the world is split into chunks, loaded around interest points (the player, agents), never around the camera;\n109\t  - terrain regenerates from the seed;\n110\t  - changed or moved objects are saved and restored when you return;\n111\t  - the pavilion sits at the center with wings branching outward, and procedural wilderness beyond it is used for streaming and encounter tests.\n112\t\n113\t## 9. Input\n114\t- **Devices:** keyboard+mouse and gamepad are always supported.\n115\t  - Each room declares a primary device, and its controls and design are built around it.\n116\t  - The secondary device gets a best-effort mapping that never changes the design.\n117\t  - On-screen prompts show the device touched last.\n118\t- **Fixed system layer that never rebinds:** pause, rewind, step, tuning panel, teleport menu, leave room and reset room.\n119\t- **Guidance and layout:** an on-screen control guide appears on entering a room. Keys are bound by physical position.\n120\t\n121\t## 10. Sound\n122\t- Everything is synthesized in code: oscillators, noise, envelopes and filters.\n123\t- The game runs without a sound device, and headless runs can write `.wav` files.\n124\t\n125\t## 11. Tools for humans\n126\t- **Boot diagnostics:**\n127\t  - numbered stages with timings, written to the terminal, a log file and the screen;\n128\t  - if graphics fail to start, a Windows pop-up shows the error and the log file's path;\n129\t  - crash reports appear on screen and in the log.\n130\t- **Tuning panel (egui):**\n131\t  - every registered parameter, grouped: physics, movement, camera, shaders, audio and simulation speed;\n132\t  - presets can be saved, exported and imported;\n133\t  - FPS, tick and frame-timing graphs;\n134\t  - possibly live shader editing.\n135\t- **Sandbox editing:** spawn, drag and delete objects, and save the room to a data file.\n136\t- **Navigation:**\n137\t  - a teleport menu and a reset button per room;\n138\t  - an info card per room saying what it demonstrates and what to try;\n139\t  - a command-line option to launch straight into a room with a given seed.\n140\t\n141\t## 12. Agent layer\n142\t- **One tool registry, three interfaces:** CLI commands, an MCP server (stdio), and later a live bridge into the running game.\n143\t- **Tools:**\n144\t  - list and describe rooms;\n145\t  - start, run, and step N ticks;\n146\t  - spawn, query and modify entities;\n147\t  - get and set parameters;\n148\t  - save, load and rewind snapshots;\n149\t  - record and replay inputs;\n150\t  - capture: screenshots, filmstrips (many frames tiled into one image), timing reports and `.wav` audio;\n151\t  - benchmark ticks per second;\n152\t  - validate and hot-load room files.\n153\t- **Rooms vs mechanics:** rooms built from existing parts are data files that hot-reload with no rebuild. New mechanics are Rust code, written from templates.\n154\t- **Rulebook:** `AGENTS.md` (Claude Code loads it, the doctrine and `PROGRESS.md` through `CLAUDE.md`). It covers how the principles apply, a short architecture map, how to add a tool or a room, and how to test and capture; `docs/REFERENCE.md` holds the full code map, every tool family, the controls and the test suites.\n155\t\n156\t## 13. Showcase roster\n157\t- **Movement & Feel Lab:**\n158\t  - courses: tightrope, slalom, timing gates, dodge gauntlet;\n159\t  - a movement-model switcher, with live input-delay and feel metrics;\n160\t  - a camera bench (sweep angle and projection on the same course);\n161\t  - a multi-floor verticality building (ladders, ledges, crawl spaces, ducking, blow a hole and drop).\n162\t- **Physics Lab:** stacking, springs and soft bodies, chains and rope bridges, conveyors, a bounce and friction gallery, destructible floors, and a stress test with a ticks/sec meter.\n163\t- **Procedural Animation Lab:** walk cycles, jointed limbs (spider or lizard), impact recoil, squash-and-stretch, secondary motion (tails, antennae), and the Character Style Bench (one character, a swingable camera, and a switch between cutout, flat-look puppet and lit puppet).\n164\t- **Visual Effects Wing:** lights and shadows, GPU particles, bloom, screen distortion, and global illumination (stretch goal).\n165\t- **Aesthetic & Filter Wing:** side-by-side comparisons of the pure style against filters (scanlines, CRT curvature, dithering, color grading, pixelation), plus a build-your-own filter stack bench.\n166\t- **Genre Wing:** bullet hell, grid stealth, a drift car and a helicopter.\n167\t\n168\t## 14. Delivery & workflow\n169\t- **Repo:** `michaelcrosato/shardfall`, stays private. No hosted site (Vercel retired\n170\t  2026-11-02): the browser build ships as one self-contained HTML file (`CLAUDE.md`).\n171\t- **Branch:** work on `main` (the GitHub default). Commit and push often, because cloud containers are temporary.\n172\t- **Windows build:** the `.exe` is cross-compiled in the cloud and sent in chat at the end of each milestone. Local WSL2 agents can build the `.exe` and launch it directly on Windows.\n173\t- **Progress log:** `docs/PROGRESS.md` is one page of current state (where things stand, what's next, open issues), so any agent can resume; `docs/HISTORY.md` is the full journal of what was built and the decisions made.\n174\t- **Build order:** sequential. Once the foundation and rulebook exist, self-contained rooms can be handed to cheaper helper agents and reviewed. This also tests the goal that smaller models can extend the engine.\n175\t- **Decisions:** ask the user only about decisions that are genuinely theirs. Otherwise decide, record the decision (in this document if it changes the design, otherwise in `docs/HISTORY.md`), and continue. Escalations follow the doctrine and are logged in `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`.\n176\t\n177\t## 15. Milestones (each ends with a working `.exe`)\n178\t1. **M1 Foundation:**\n179\t   - workspace and crates;\n180\t   - window, wgpu on Vulkan, and boot diagnostics;\n181\t   - a headless simulation loop;\n182\t   - lavapipe capture (screenshots) working in the cloud;\n183\t   - Windows cross-compile verified;\n184\t   - `AGENTS.md` and `PROGRESS.md`.\n185\t\n186\t   *Done when:* the `.exe` opens and shows the boot log and a lit test scene, the headless CLI runs ticks, and capture produces a PNG.\n187\t2. **M2 Core gameplay:**\n188\t   - rapier3d integration and tile-based levels with heights;\n189\t   - character puppet v1;\n190\t   - movement models v1 (instant and momentum);\n191\t   - the camera system, with all parameters live;\n192\t   - keyboard+mouse and gamepad input, with the system layer;\n193\t   - the egui tuning panel;\n194\t   - time controls (pause, step, speed, rewind);\n195\t   - agent CLI and MCP server v1.\n196\t\n197\t   *Done when:* the player can walk, jump onto boxes, climb a ladder, blow a hole in a floor and drop through, tweak camera and movement live, and rewind.\n198\t3. **M3 World & rooms:**\n199\t   - chunk streaming, the pavilion and the wilderness;\n200\t   - the room framework: enter/exit, input switching, control guide, info card, reset, teleport, launch-into-room;\n201\t   - room data files with hot reload;\n202\t   - sandbox editing;\n203\t   - synth sound v1;\n204\t   - filmstrip capture.\n205\t4. **M4:** Movement & Feel Lab.\n206\t5. **M5:** Physics Lab.\n207\t6. **M6:** Procedural Animation Lab, including the Character Style Bench.\n208\t7. **M7:** Visual Effects Wing.\n209\t8. **M8:** Aesthetic & Filter Wing.\n210\t9. **M9:** Genre Wing.\n211\t10. **M10:** Browser build, live agent bridge, and a polish pass.\n212\t\n213\t## 16. Out of scope for now\n214\t- Determinism across machines.\n215\t- Mac support and other browsers.\n216\t- Compatibility work.\n217\t- Code signing.\n218\t- Performance optimization beyond \"runs well on a modern PC\".\n219\t- GitHub automation.\n220\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33b9a197-b2fe-42e5-9a43-b32ae2dde858","uuid":"c6ece7f2-175b-4f53-a2ae-ed0f250d8ceb","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:04.253Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/.scout/shardfall/docs/DESIGN.md","content":"# Shardfall — Engine & Showcase Design Plan\n\n\"Shardfall\" is the game and repository name. \"Pavilion\" is the engine's playable tech demo.\nThis document is the source of truth for what we are building and why; the principles are in `docs/DOCTRINE.md`.\nIf a pasted version of this plan in chat differs from this file, the pasted version wins; update this file to match.\n\n## 1. What we're building\nAn AI-first game engine, plus a living tech-demo showcase built on it, written in Rust. Everything you see and hear is generated from code.\n\nThe showcase is a continuous pavilion of themed wings and test rooms set inside an infinite streaming world. It serves as a personal evaluation lab and as living documentation.\n\nThe engine is meant to be reused for many different games. We don't know yet what game we're making, so flexibility wins over polish.\n\n## 2. Principles\n`docs/DOCTRINE.md` sets the principles and the escalation process, and outranks this document.\n`AGENTS.md` says how the doctrine applies to this repo and lists the working rules under it:\ngameplay first, use tools, run well on a modern PC, approximations that feel right, check\nselectively, token efficiency.\n\n## 3. Platform & tech\n- **Where it runs:**\n  - Native desktop app.\n  - The human plays on Windows 11 (RTX 3060 Ti at work, RTX 4070 Super at home).\n  - Agents build, run and test on Linux (cloud containers, WSL2 Ubuntu).\n- **Language:** Rust. The stable toolchain is pinned with `rust-toolchain.toml`, and `Cargo.lock` is committed.\n- **Graphics:** wgpu, which implements the WebGPU API.\n  - Vulkan is the default backend on Windows and Linux.\n  - DX12 can be selected with a startup setting.\n  - No OpenGL or WebGL.\n- **Headless rendering for agents:** Mesa lavapipe (software Vulkan) on machines without a GPU. Same engine code, different driver.\n- **Physics:** rapier3d. Coordinates are f32, with a one-line switch to f64 (`rapier3d-f64`) if we ever see jitter far from the origin.\n- **Other libraries:** winit for window and input, gilrs for gamepads, cpal for audio output (with our own synthesizer), egui for the debug UI.\n- **Browser build:** a late milestone. The same code compiles to WebAssembly and WebGPU. Library choices keep this possible, but we don't maintain it until then.\n- **Assets:** no image, model, texture or audio files. Geometry, surfaces, effects and sound are all generated from code. Fonts are allowed, for UI and in-world text.\n\n## 4. World & camera\n- **The world is fully 3D.** \"2D top-down\" is a camera preset, not an engine limit.\n- **Camera parameters are live:**\n  - tilt, from 90° (straight down) to 0° (side view);\n  - rotation;\n  - distance/zoom;\n  - orthographic or perspective projection;\n  - field of view.\n\n  They can be changed mid-game from the tuning panel, each room saves its own defaults, and game code can animate the camera.\n- **Standard camera helpers:**\n  - fade or cut away geometry between the camera and the player;\n  - hide upper floors while the player is below them;\n  - mouse aim targets the point on the ground plane at the player's height;\n  - movement input is relative to the camera's rotation.\n- **Default look:** tilted to about 60–75°, stylized with flat or cel shading and outlines. A 90° orthographic view gives the clean 2D look.\n\n## 5. Rendering\n- **Pipeline:** a real 3D renderer with depth, MSAA, HDR and a post-processing chain.\n- **Procedural geometry:** boxes, rounded boxes, cylinders, spheres, capsules, extruded shapes, tile terrain, tubes and ropes.\n- **Analytic SDF primitives** (spheres, capsules, etc.) are pixel-perfect at any zoom, and can blend smoothly into each other for organic shapes.\n- **Swappable styles** per room or per object: flat 2D look, cel/toon, or fully lit.\n- **Lighting:** dynamic lights with real shadows, emissive surfaces and bloom.\n- **GPU compute is cosmetic only** (particles and similar). Anything that affects gameplay runs on the CPU.\n- **Text:** in-world text is rendered by the engine from a font; UI text goes through egui.\n\n## 6. Characters\n- **Puppet model:** an invisible skeleton, driven by procedural animation, with parts attached to the bones.\n  - **Procedural animation:** walk cycles driven by speed, foot IK to the ground, leaning into turns, recoil, squash-and-stretch, and secondary motion.\n  - **Parts:** mostly spheres and capsules, so silhouettes read as clean 2D shapes from any camera angle.\n- **One motion, many looks:**\n  - a cutout (flat 2D art on a camera-facing card, for fixed-camera designs);\n  - a puppet with a flat 2D look;\n  - a lit puppet.\n- **Camera-aware rules** recover 2D \"cheats\", such as eyes that stay visible or a body that leans toward the viewer. Stepped animation (\"on twos\") is optional.\n- **Everything is a parameter:** proportions, colors and limb count are sliders.\n\n## 7. Physics, height & movement\n- **Real verticality:**\n  - jumping gaps, climbing onto boxes, ladders, ledges and falls;\n  - crawling under things and ducking projectiles;\n  - multi-floor buildings with destructible floor tiles (blow a hole, drop to the floor below).\n- **Flat rooms** lock motion to a plane (bullet hell). **Side-view rooms** are a camera setting plus an axis lock, so Celeste-style rooms are possible.\n- **High-count projectiles and bullets** use a lightweight custom system, not physics bodies.\n- **Movement models** plug in per character, can be switched from a dropdown per room, and every number in them is tunable:\n  - **Instant:** immediate response, with a hold-to-slow focus button.\n  - **Momentum:** acceleration, skidding, air control.\n  - **Grid/tile:** step-by-step movement.\n  - **Committed animations:** moves play out fully once started.\n  - **Vehicle:** grip and drift.\n  - **Flight:** banking and inertia.\n  - **Special states:** climbing and swimming.\n\n  Each room marks the model it was designed for. Other models can be tried wherever it makes sense.\n- **Tick rate** defaults to 60 per second, with 120 and 240 selectable. Input delay is measurable, and vsync and smoothing can be toggled.\n\n## 8. Simulation architecture\n- **Headless-first:** the simulation core is a library with no window, graphics or audio dependencies. It runs in two ways:\n  - inside the game, on its own thread; the renderer observes and interpolates, and neither blocks the other;\n  - as a headless command-line program at maximum speed.\n- **Time control:** fixed tick rate, speed control from slow motion to maximum, pause and single-step.\n- **Repeatability (the cheap version):** the same machine, build, seed and recorded inputs give the same result.\n  - It exists to support rewind and bug replays.\n  - It is not guaranteed across machines.\n  - If it ever gets in the way of gameplay or performance, gameplay wins.\n- **Time travel:**\n  - periodic snapshots plus an input log;\n  - rewind restores the nearest snapshot and re-simulates forward;\n  - history is limited by memory (adjustable);\n  - acting after a rewind starts a new timeline;\n  - snapshots can be saved to files.\n- **Streaming:**\n  - the world is split into chunks, loaded around interest points (the player, agents), never around the camera;\n  - terrain regenerates from the seed;\n  - changed or moved objects are saved and restored when you return;\n  - the pavilion sits at the center with wings branching outward, and procedural wilderness beyond it is used for streaming and encounter tests.\n\n## 9. Input\n- **Devices:** keyboard+mouse and gamepad are always supported.\n  - Each room declares a primary device, and its controls and design are built around it.\n  - The secondary device gets a best-effort mapping that never changes the design.\n  - On-screen prompts show the device touched last.\n- **Fixed system layer that never rebinds:** pause, rewind, step, tuning panel, teleport menu, leave room and reset room.\n- **Guidance and layout:** an on-screen control guide appears on entering a room. Keys are bound by physical position.\n\n## 10. Sound\n- Everything is synthesized in code: oscillators, noise, envelopes and filters.\n- The game runs without a sound device, and headless runs can write `.wav` files.\n\n## 11. Tools for humans\n- **Boot diagnostics:**\n  - numbered stages with timings, written to the terminal, a log file and the screen;\n  - if graphics fail to start, a Windows pop-up shows the error and the log file's path;\n  - crash reports appear on screen and in the log.\n- **Tuning panel (egui):**\n  - every registered parameter, grouped: physics, movement, camera, shaders, audio and simulation speed;\n  - presets can be saved, exported and imported;\n  - FPS, tick and frame-timing graphs;\n  - possibly live shader editing.\n- **Sandbox editing:** spawn, drag and delete objects, and save the room to a data file.\n- **Navigation:**\n  - a teleport menu and a reset button per room;\n  - an info card per room saying what it demonstrates and what to try;\n  - a command-line option to launch straight into a room with a given seed.\n\n## 12. Agent layer\n- **One tool registry, three interfaces:** CLI commands, an MCP server (stdio), and later a live bridge into the running game.\n- **Tools:**\n  - list and describe rooms;\n  - start, run, and step N ticks;\n  - spawn, query and modify entities;\n  - get and set parameters;\n  - save, load and rewind snapshots;\n  - record and replay inputs;\n  - capture: screenshots, filmstrips (many frames tiled into one image), timing reports and `.wav` audio;\n  - benchmark ticks per second;\n  - validate and hot-load room files.\n- **Rooms vs mechanics:** rooms built from existing parts are data files that hot-reload with no rebuild. New mechanics are Rust code, written from templates.\n- **Rulebook:** `AGENTS.md` (Claude Code loads it, the doctrine and `PROGRESS.md` through `CLAUDE.md`). It covers how the principles apply, a short architecture map, how to add a tool or a room, and how to test and capture; `docs/REFERENCE.md` holds the full code map, every tool family, the controls and the test suites.\n\n## 13. Showcase roster\n- **Movement & Feel Lab:**\n  - courses: tightrope, slalom, timing gates, dodge gauntlet;\n  - a movement-model switcher, with live input-delay and feel metrics;\n  - a camera bench (sweep angle and projection on the same course);\n  - a multi-floor verticality building (ladders, ledges, crawl spaces, ducking, blow a hole and drop).\n- **Physics Lab:** stacking, springs and soft bodies, chains and rope bridges, conveyors, a bounce and friction gallery, destructible floors, and a stress test with a ticks/sec meter.\n- **Procedural Animation Lab:** walk cycles, jointed limbs (spider or lizard), impact recoil, squash-and-stretch, secondary motion (tails, antennae), and the Character Style Bench (one character, a swingable camera, and a switch between cutout, flat-look puppet and lit puppet).\n- **Visual Effects Wing:** lights and shadows, GPU particles, bloom, screen distortion, and global illumination (stretch goal).\n- **Aesthetic & Filter Wing:** side-by-side comparisons of the pure style against filters (scanlines, CRT curvature, dithering, color grading, pixelation), plus a build-your-own filter stack bench.\n- **Genre Wing:** bullet hell, grid stealth, a drift car and a helicopter.\n\n## 14. Delivery & workflow\n- **Repo:** `michaelcrosato/shardfall`, stays private. No hosted site (Vercel retired\n  2026-11-02): the browser build ships as one self-contained HTML file (`CLAUDE.md`).\n- **Branch:** work on `main` (the GitHub default). Commit and push often, because cloud containers are temporary.\n- **Windows build:** the `.exe` is cross-compiled in the cloud and sent in chat at the end of each milestone. Local WSL2 agents can build the `.exe` and launch it directly on Windows.\n- **Progress log:** `docs/PROGRESS.md` is one page of current state (where things stand, what's next, open issues), so any agent can resume; `docs/HISTORY.md` is the full journal of what was built and the decisions made.\n- **Build order:** sequential. Once the foundation and rulebook exist, self-contained rooms can be handed to cheaper helper agents and reviewed. This also tests the goal that smaller models can extend the engine.\n- **Decisions:** ask the user only about decisions that are genuinely theirs. Otherwise decide, record the decision (in this document if it changes the design, otherwise in `docs/HISTORY.md`), and continue. Escalations follow the doctrine and are logged in `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`.\n\n## 15. Milestones (each ends with a working `.exe`)\n1. **M1 Foundation:**\n   - workspace and crates;\n   - window, wgpu on Vulkan, and boot diagnostics;\n   - a headless simulation loop;\n   - lavapipe capture (screenshots) working in the cloud;\n   - Windows cross-compile verified;\n   - `AGENTS.md` and `PROGRESS.md`.\n\n   *Done when:* the `.exe` opens and shows the boot log and a lit test scene, the headless CLI runs ticks, and capture produces a PNG.\n2. **M2 Core gameplay:**\n   - rapier3d integration and tile-based levels with heights;\n   - character puppet v1;\n   - movement models v1 (instant and momentum);\n   - the camera system, with all parameters live;\n   - keyboard+mouse and gamepad input, with the system layer;\n   - the egui tuning panel;\n   - time controls (pause, step, speed, rewind);\n   - agent CLI and MCP server v1.\n\n   *Done when:* the player can walk, jump onto boxes, climb a ladder, blow a hole in a floor and drop through, tweak camera and movement live, and rewind.\n3. **M3 World & rooms:**\n   - chunk streaming, the pavilion and the wilderness;\n   - the room framework: enter/exit, input switching, control guide, info card, reset, teleport, launch-into-room;\n   - room data files with hot reload;\n   - sandbox editing;\n   - synth sound v1;\n   - filmstrip capture.\n4. **M4:** Movement & Feel Lab.\n5. **M5:** Physics Lab.\n6. **M6:** Procedural Animation Lab, including the Character Style Bench.\n7. **M7:** Visual Effects Wing.\n8. **M8:** Aesthetic & Filter Wing.\n9. **M9:** Genre Wing.\n10. **M10:** Browser build, live agent bridge, and a polish pass.\n\n## 16. Out of scope for now\n- Determinism across machines.\n- Mac support and other browsers.\n- Compatibility work.\n- Code signing.\n- Performance optimization beyond \"runs well on a modern PC\".\n- GitHub automation.\n","numLines":220,"startLine":1,"totalLines":220}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015prNaSsyzmX7xtRVYS97XB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
