{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjCnK2kCtmEC2oR4Dsmi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011G1XQ8Gn3GqupzuugXy67h","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/planner-exit-interview.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What did you like most about this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"The 'This computer' section. It says what is installed, which versions, what has no real device and which stand-in to use (a stubbed getGamepads, phone profiles, OfflineAudioContext, two pages). Because it was specific I could run the real checks myself and tell the builders what works here: the engine builds in about 3 minutes, the single-file page runs from file://, and a pad stub reaches the wasm build. I also liked that the goals are concrete and testable: attract at 10 seconds, music at 10%, a touch-only message, a named harness that decides pass or fail. A plan can be held to those.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was confusing?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Five things. (1) 'Follow lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own': the library says nothing changes outside a session and offers no standalone network, so I couldn't tell whether a game on its own needs online play, couch play or just solo. I chose solo plus couch co-op and said so in the plan. (2) TASK.md says to build on START_HERE.md, whose route says to start from the starter game and skip Shardfall's code, but the engine's own game is already an action RPG and the source is one too. I overrode the guide and chose to fork that module, but the prompt doesn't say whether that counts as 'making a new game'. (3) 'Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters...)' sits next to 'Build an original game', and the title has to riff on the source's name. I resolved it as: mechanics, structure and tone carry over, while every name, character and level is new, with a banned-word test. (4) The WebGPU flags in TASK.md didn't work here: with --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader alone the GPU device was lost and the canvas was blank. The flags in the engine's docs, --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader, drew the game. (5) STAGE.md says to leave out TASK.md's file names, but it took a moment to decide whether that also covers fixed names like the exit interview file. I left them all out.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Sizing it. A complete action RPG with four classes, a story chapter, loot systems, a full 10-foot UI, synthesized music and voices, plus a four-player shared world over a link limited to 20 JSON messages a second, has to be something builders of unknown strength can finish without stubs. That is why the plan has a fixed build order, a page that can ship from M1 on, and a cut list that removes whole features. The netcode was the hardest single decision: the engine's co-op plan is 8,000 to 12,000 lines, and I had to find a much smaller model (host authority, guests owning their own position, pure logic testable in one process) that still meets the hub's limits and its server and host-leave checks. I checked the page side of it by running real wasm copies through the harness; the Rust netcode itself is untested design.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What would you change in this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Replace the WebGPU flag line with one that draws the game here, and warn that software rendering runs at about 2 to 3 fps with the sim throttled to about 5 ticks a second, so nobody plans to play-test in the browser. Say plainly what multiplayer 'on its own' must mean: solo, couch or online. Say whether reusing the engine's existing game code is allowed for a source in the same genre, since that decision changes the whole plan. Tell the planner who will build from the plan and with what effort, because a plan for a top model and one for a small model should differ. Give http.server's -u flag in the server example, because without it the port line stays stuck in a pipe buffer and a script reading it hangs, which cost me a 400-second timeout. Finally, state the 'don't recreate the source' boundary directly, rather than leaving a title riff and 'capture its world and characters' to imply where it lies.\"\n  }\n]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1325,"cache_read_input_tokens":515233,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1325},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33b9a197-b2fe-42e5-9a43-b32ae2dde858","uuid":"f592c74d-16a0-4689-b4c9-4da0267f65d5","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:09:47.401Z","request_id":"req_011CgjCnJWEuNNsNXbAGtuNA","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_011G1XQ8Gn3GqupzuugXy67h":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211941__xf7be7f/work/planner-exit-interview.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What did you like most about this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"The 'This computer' section. It says what is installed, which versions, what has no real device and which stand-in to use (a stubbed getGamepads, phone profiles, OfflineAudioContext, two pages). Because it was specific I could run the real checks myself and tell the builders what works here: the engine builds in about 3 minutes, the single-file page runs from file://, and a pad stub reaches the wasm build. I also liked that the goals are concrete and testable: attract at 10 seconds, music at 10%, a touch-only message, a named harness that decides pass or fail. A plan can be held to those.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was confusing?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Five things. (1) 'Follow lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own': the library says nothing changes outside a session and offers no standalone network, so I couldn't tell whether a game on its own needs online play, couch play or just solo. I chose solo plus couch co-op and said so in the plan. (2) TASK.md says to build on START_HERE.md, whose route says to start from the starter game and skip Shardfall's code, but the engine's own game is already an action RPG and the source is one too. I overrode the guide and chose to fork that module, but the prompt doesn't say whether that counts as 'making a new game'. (3) 'Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters...)' sits next to 'Build an original game', and the title has to riff on the source's name. I resolved it as: mechanics, structure and tone carry over, while every name, character and level is new, with a banned-word test. (4) The WebGPU flags in TASK.md didn't work here: with --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader alone the GPU device was lost and the canvas was blank. The flags in the engine's docs, --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader, drew the game. (5) STAGE.md says to leave out TASK.md's file names, but it took a moment to decide whether that also covers fixed names like the exit interview file. I left them all out.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Sizing it. A complete action RPG with four classes, a story chapter, loot systems, a full 10-foot UI, synthesized music and voices, plus a four-player shared world over a link limited to 20 JSON messages a second, has to be something builders of unknown strength can finish without stubs. That is why the plan has a fixed build order, a page that can ship from M1 on, and a cut list that removes whole features. The netcode was the hardest single decision: the engine's co-op plan is 8,000 to 12,000 lines, and I had to find a much smaller model (host authority, guests owning their own position, pure logic testable in one process) that still meets the hub's limits and its server and host-leave checks. I checked the page side of it by running real wasm copies through the harness; the Rust netcode itself is untested design.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What would you change in this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Replace the WebGPU flag line with one that draws the game here, and warn that software rendering runs at about 2 to 3 fps with the sim throttled to about 5 ticks a second, so nobody plans to play-test in the browser. Say plainly what multiplayer 'on its own' must mean: solo, couch or online. Say whether reusing the engine's existing game code is allowed for a source in the same genre, since that decision changes the whole plan. Tell the planner who will build from the plan and with what effort, because a plan for a top model and one for a small model should differ. Give http.server's -u flag in the server example, because without it the port line stays stuck in a pipe buffer and a script reading it hangs, which cost me a 400-second timeout. Finally, state the 'don't recreate the source' boundary directly, rather than leaving a title riff and 'capture its world and characters' to imply where it lies.\"\n  }\n]\n"}}}
